window.WORKBOOK={"weeks":[{"week":2,"problems":[{"id":"w02-p1","questions":[{"expected":"620*730/1000","units":"million m³","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["For one cell, depth times area is the volume of rain on it. Sample $d$ at each upper-right corner, add the four depths, and multiply by $\\Delta A\\approx730$ km$^2$.","The corners are $(1,1)$, $(1,2)$, $(2,1)$, $(2,2)$. Convert: 1 mm of rain on 1 km$^2$ is 1000 m$^3$, or 0.001 million m$^3$."],"id":"w02-p1-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the two-by-two block of cells with crosses at the four upper-right corners, labeled (1,1), (1,2), (2,1), and (2,2), and arrows showing that d grows eastward and northward. Right: the rising curve of d along the line y = 1, with a dashed rectangle on each cell whose height is the value at the right end of the cell","caption":"Because $d$ grows in both $x$ and $y$, each upper-right corner is the wettest point of its cell; multiply each corner depth by $\\Delta A\\approx730$ km$^2$.","file":"w02-p1-a"}},{"expected":"430*730/1000","units":"million m³","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Sample $d$ at the center of each cell instead of a corner.","The midpoints are $(0.5,0.5)$, $(0.5,1.5)$, $(1.5,0.5)$, $(1.5,1.5)$; convert with mm $\\times$ km$^2\\times0.001=$ million m$^3$."],"id":"w02-p1-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the two-by-two block of cells with a dot at the center of each cell, labeled (0.5, 0.5), (0.5, 1.5), (1.5, 0.5), and (1.5, 1.5). Right: a thin slab of water 1 km by 1 km by 1 mm deep, labeled as 1000 cubic meters, or 0.001 million cubic meters","caption":"Sample $d$ at the four cell centers, and convert with 1 mm on 1 km$^2$ $=1000$ m$^3=0.001$ million m$^3$.","file":"w02-p1-b"}},{"expected":"440*730/1000","units":"million m³","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["In cell units, integrate $d$ over $[0,2]\\times[0,2]$; each unit square of the $(x,y)$-plane is one cell of 730 km$^2$.","Integrate in $y$ first; you will need $\\int_0^2x^2\\,dx=8/3$. Size check: a few hundred million m$^3$, and below your answer to (a)."],"id":"w02-p1-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the block of cells with a vertical segment at a fixed x running from y = 0 to y = 2. Right: the cross-section of the rain solid above that segment, a trapezoid whose height rises linearly from 50 + 30x squared at y = 0 to 90 + 30x squared at y = 2","caption":"At fixed $x$, the depth is linear in $y$, so the cross-section $A(x)=\\int_0^2 d\\,dy$ is a trapezoid; then integrate $A(x)$ from $x=0$ to $2$.","file":"w02-p1-c"}}]},{"id":"w02-p2","questions":[{"expected":"0","units":"","kind":"choice","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Look along one horizontal line $y=$ constant at a time, and compare the cold piece with the warm piece.","$T_1<0$ only for $x<-0.3$, because the factor $1-y^2$ is never negative on $B$."],"options":["Positive","Negative","Zero, because $T_1$ changes sign","Impossible to tell without computing"],"id":"w02-p2-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the box B with a horizontal line y = c, cold shading left of x = −0.3 and warm shading to its right. Right: the graph of T1 along that line, a straight line crossing zero at x = −0.3, with a cold region marked minus below the axis from −1 to −0.3 and a warm region marked plus above the axis from −0.3 to 1","caption":"Along each line $y=c$, $T_1$ is a straight line that is negative on $-1\\le x<-0.3$ and positive on $-0.3<x\\le1$; compare the two signed areas.","file":"w02-p2-a"}},{"expected":"0.9+0.6*(2/3)","units":"°C","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Over the symmetric box, the $x^3$ and $y$ terms cancel: pair $(x,y)$ with $(-x,y)$, or with $(x,-y)$.","The constant $0.9$ averages to itself; then average $1-x^2$ over $-1\\le x\\le1$. Size check: since $0\\le1-x^2\\le1$, the index is between 0.9 and 1.5 °C."],"id":"w02-p2-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the box B with a point (x, y), its mirror image (−x, y) across the y-axis, where x cubed flips sign, and its mirror image (x, −y) across the x-axis, where y flips sign. Right: the parabola 1 − x squared over −1 to 1 with the area under it shaded, and a note that its average equals the shaded area divided by 2","caption":"Mirror pairs cancel the $1.6x^3$ and $-0.8y$ terms; what remains is the constant $0.9$ plus $0.6$ times the average of $1-x^2$.","file":"w02-p2-b"}},{"expected":"3*exp(-1)","units":"°C","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["$T_3$ is smallest where $x^2+y^2$ is largest on the box, at the corners.","On $B$, $-(x^2+y^2)/2\\ge-1$, and $e^s$ is increasing in $s$."],"id":"w02-p2-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the box B shaded by the warm pool, hottest at the center, with dashed circles of constant x squared plus y squared, the four corners marked, and the distance square root of 2 from the center to a corner. Right: the increasing curve 3e to the s for s from −1 at the corners to 0 at the center","caption":"$x^2+y^2$ is largest, $2$, at the corners, so $s=-(x^2+y^2)/2$ runs from $-1$ to $0$ on $B$, and $3e^s$ is smallest where $s$ is smallest.","file":"w02-p2-c"}}]},{"id":"w02-p3","questions":[{"expected":"4/3*66*112^2","units":"m³","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["By symmetry, the diagonals split the base into 8 congruent triangles; on $0\\le y\\le x\\le112$, $h=66(1-x/112)$.","On that triangle, integrate in $y$ first: the inner integral multiplies the height by the slice length $x$."],"id":"w02-p3-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: top view of the square base with the triangle 0 ≤ y ≤ x shaded and a vertical segment at a fixed x running from y = 0 up to the diagonal y = x. Right: the slice of the pyramid above that segment, a rectangle of length x and constant height 66(1 − x/112)","caption":"On the triangle $0\\le y\\le x\\le112$ the height $66(1-x/112)$ does not depend on $y$, so the inner integral is that height times the slice length $x$.","file":"w02-p3-a"}},{"expected":"16.5*(168^2+112^2+56^2)","units":"m³","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Treat the staircase as stacked square slabs 16.5 m thick. Level $k$ reaches out to where the smooth face is at height $16.5k$ m, the top of that level.","The levels have sides 168, 112, 56, and 0 m; the top level shrinks to a point."],"id":"w02-p3-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Side view of the smooth pyramid with the inscribed four-level staircase inside it. Dotted lines at heights 16.5, 33, 49.5, and 66 m meet the smooth face at the top of each level, and each level is labeled with its side: 168, 112, and 56 m, and 0 for the top level, which shrinks to a point","caption":"Each inscribed level is a square slab 16.5 m thick whose side is set where the smooth face reaches the top of that level.","file":"w02-p3-b"}},{"expected":"16.5*(224^2+168^2+112^2+56^2)","units":"m³","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Level $k$ now reaches out to where the smooth face is at height $16.5(k-1)$ m, the bottom of that level.","The levels have sides 224, 168, 112, and 56 m. Size check: more than your answer to (a), but less than the $224\\times224\\times66$ m$^3$ box, about 3.3 million m$^3$."],"id":"w02-p3-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Side view of the smooth pyramid with the circumscribed four-level staircase drawn dashed around it. Dotted lines at heights 0, 16.5, 33, and 49.5 m meet the smooth face at the bottom of each level, and each level is labeled with its side: 224, 168, 112, and 56 m","caption":"Each circumscribed level is a square slab 16.5 m thick whose side is set where the smooth face is at the bottom of that level.","file":"w02-p3-c"}}]},{"id":"w02-p4","questions":[{"expected":"sqrt(1-0.36)","units":"","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Solve the shoreline equation $|y|=1-x^2$ for $x$.","Keep the root with $x\\ge0$, because the lake lies upstream of the dam."],"id":"w02-p4-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Plan view of the lake D between the shorelines y = 1 − x squared and y = −(1 − x squared), with a horizontal segment at y = 0.36 running from the dam at x = 0 to the upper shoreline, where the end point is marked x = ?, next to notes that both shorelines say |y| = 1 − x squared and that x should be kept nonnegative","caption":"In the Type II description, a horizontal line at height $y$ runs from the dam $x=0$ to the shoreline $|y|=1-x^2$; solve that for $x$.","file":"w02-p4-a"}},{"expected":"8/15*(3/4*1874)*0.14","units":"km³","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The volume is $LWH$ times a scaled integral of the depth profile, so first find $LW$ from the published area.","In scaled units the area integral is $4/3$, so $\\frac43LW=1874$ km$^2$; then multiply $LW$ by $H=0.14$ km and by the scaled volume integral. Size check: the depth never exceeds $H$, so $V<1874\\times0.14\\approx260$ km$^3$."],"id":"w02-p4-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: plan view of the lake with a vertical segment at a fixed x running from y = −(1 − x squared) to y = 1 − x squared. Right: the depth across that segment, a tent shape that is 0 at y = ±c and peaks at c = 1 − x squared at y = 0","caption":"For the Type I volume integral, the depth $1-x^2-|y|$ across a line of fixed $x$ is a tent of height and half-width $c=1-x^2$; use the area to find $LW$ first.","file":"w02-p4-b"}},{"expected":"(1-exp(-4))/4","units":"","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Reverse the order: at height $z$, the water spans $-z\\le y\\le z$.","After the $y$-integral, substitute $u=4z^2$. The cross-section has area 1 in scaled units."],"id":"w02-p4-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"The V-shaped water cross-section at the dam between the bed z = |y| and the surface z = 1, shaded darker near the bottom, with a horizontal segment at height z running from y = −z to y = z, and a dashed vertical sounding from z = |y| up to 1 labeled as having no antiderivative","caption":"Integrate along horizontal lines: at height $z$ the water spans $-z\\le y\\le z$, and $e^{-4z^2}$ is constant along that line.","file":"w02-p4-c"}}]},{"id":"w02-p5","questions":[{"expected":"16","units":"million km²","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["By symmetry, count the cells in one quarter of the grid and multiply by 4.","A cell lies entirely inside when its corner farthest from the pole is within $r\\approx2.88$ thousand km of the pole."],"id":"w02-p5-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"One quarter of the grid, three by three cells of 1000 km, with the 220 DU contour as a quarter circle around the pole. A dot marks the far corner of every cell, and the example cell from 1 to 2 in both directions is highlighted with a dashed line from the pole to its far corner (2, 2); notes give the test (i+1) squared plus (j+1) squared at most r squared, with r squared about 8.28","caption":"A cell lies entirely inside the circle exactly when its far corner does; test each far corner against $r^2\\approx8.28$.","file":"w02-p5-a"}},{"expected":"36","units":"million km²","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["A cell meets the hole when its corner nearest the pole is closer than $r$ to the pole. Size check: the upper sum is more than the true area, 26.0 million km$^2$.","In one quarter, even the cell from $(2,2)$ to $(3,3)$ counts, because $2^2+2^2=8<r^2\\approx8.28$."],"id":"w02-p5-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"One quarter of the grid, three by three cells of 1000 km, with the 220 DU contour as a quarter circle around the pole. A dot marks the near corner of every cell, and the example cell from 2 to 3 in both directions is highlighted with a dashed line from the pole to its near corner (2, 2); notes give the test i squared plus j squared less than r squared","caption":"A cell meets the hole exactly when its near corner is closer than $r$ to the pole; test each near corner against $r^2\\approx8.28$.","file":"w02-p5-b"}},{"expected":"11","units":"million km²","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Each cell now has area 0.25 million km$^2$. The gap is the total area of the cells that meet the hole but are not entirely inside.","In one quarter, 20 of the 500 km cells lie entirely inside; count the cells that meet the hole with the nearest-corner test."],"id":"w02-p5-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"One quarter of the grid with 500 km cells and the quarter-circle contour, with three example cells shaded: one entirely inside the circle, one crossed by the circle, and one outside, and a key saying that only the crossed cells count in the upper sum but not the lower sum","caption":"The gap between the sums is the area of the cells the contour crosses; count them with the near-corner and far-corner tests, at $0.25$ million km$^2$ each.","file":"w02-p5-c"}}]}]},{"week":3,"problems":[{"id":"w03-p1","questions":[{"expected":["4","1"],"units":"","kind":"vector","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The crest is the point that lies on both curves.","Setting $4y^2=5.5-1.5y$ gives $8y^2+3y-11=0$; one root is negative, below the ground."],"id":"w03-p1-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"The dune cross-section with the windward face x = 4y^2 and the slip face x = 5.5 - 1.5y. The crest, where the two curves meet above the ground, is circled. Dashed extensions of both curves below the ground cross a second time, also circled.","caption":"The crest lies on both curves. Extended below the ground (dashed), the same two equations cross a second time, at a root that is not on the dune.","file":"w03-p1-a"}},{"expected":"41/12*110^2","units":"m²","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["In the order $dx\\,dy$, a horizontal slice at height $y$ runs from $x=4y^2$ to $x=5.5-1.5y$.","Integrate over $0\\le y\\le1$, then multiply by $110^2$ to get square meters. Size check: expect more than the triangle with the same three corners, about 33,000 m²."],"id":"w03-p1-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"The cross-section with one thin horizontal slice at height y, starting on the windward face x = 4y^2 and ending on the slip face x = 5.5 - 1.5y, with thickness dy. An arrow shows y running from the base up to the crest. Heights are drawn twice as tall as widths.","caption":"In the order $dx\\,dy$, each horizontal slice runs from $x=4y^2$ to $x=5.5-1.5y$, and $y$ runs from the base up to the crest.","file":"w03-p1-b"}},{"expected":"15/41*110","units":"m","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The first moment $\\iint_D y\\,dA$ adds up height × area over thin horizontal strips; the geologist’s sum is this first moment. Divide it by $A$.","Combined, the first moment is $\\int_0^1 y\\,(5.5-1.5y-4y^2)\\,dy$. Divide by $A$ in model units, then convert with 1 unit $=110$ m."],"id":"w03-p1-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Top: the cross-section split at x = 4 into two pieces, with a vertical slice in each, matching the geologist’s two integrals. Bottom: the same region with one horizontal slice at height y.","caption":"The geologist’s two pieces of vertical slices fill the same region as one stack of horizontal slices. Each horizontal slice at height $y$ adds $y$ times its area to the first moment.","file":"w03-p1-c"}}]},{"id":"w03-p2","questions":[{"expected":"90*52*35","units":"ft³","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The tank is a box: integrate $1$ over $0\\le x\\le90$, $0\\le y\\le52$, $0\\le z\\le35$ (ft). On a box this is length × width × height.","Size check: the published 1.2 million gallons is about 160,000 ft³."],"id":"w03-p2-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Oblique drawing of the tank as a box, 0 to 90 ft in x, 0 to 52 ft in y, and 0 to 35 ft in z, with one small box dV = dx dy dz inside.","caption":"Integrating $1$ over $B$ adds up the volume of every small box $dV$; the limits are the three edges of the tank.","file":"w03-p2-a"}},{"expected":"90*52*0.1*(35^2/2-20*35)","units":"°C·ft³","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The integrand depends only on $z$, so the $x$ and $y$ integrals just give a factor of $90\\times52$.","Compute $0.1\\int_0^{35}(z-20)\\,dz$, and check its sign against your prediction."],"id":"w03-p2-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: side view of the tank with a thin horizontal slab at height z, of area 90 by 52 square feet and thickness dz. Right: the graph of T - T0 = 0.1(z - 20) against height z, positive (shaded warm) above z = 20 ft and negative (shaded cool) below.","caption":"$T-T_0$ is the same all across each horizontal slab, so the $x$ and $y$ integrals give the slab area $90\\times52$ ft$^2$. The sign of $0.1(z-20)$ changes at $z=20$ ft.","file":"w03-p2-b"}},{"expected":"2*12.42/(2*pi)","units":"h","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Reverse the order: for $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/2$, the height runs from $y=0$ to $y=\\sin\\theta$.","The band has scaled height 1, so the integral is already the average, in radians of phase; 1 rad $=12.42/(2\\pi)$ h. Size check: the answer is less than half of the 12.42 h cycle."],"id":"w03-p2-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Two copies of the region under y = sin(theta) for theta from 0 to pi/2. Left: a horizontal slice at height y from theta = arcsin y to theta = pi/2. Right: a vertical slice at phase theta from y = 0 up to y = sin(theta).","caption":"The same region read two ways. As written, $\\theta$ runs from $\\arcsin y$ to $\\pi/2$; reversed, $y$ runs from $0$ up to $\\sin\\theta$.","file":"w03-p2-c"}}]},{"id":"w03-p3","questions":[{"expected":"10*0.5*0.3*8/15","units":"km³","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Project onto the $yz$-plane: for each $(y,z)$ with $y^2\\le z\\le1$, $x$ runs from $0$ to $1-z$.","Multiply the scaled volume by $LWH=10\\times0.5\\times0.3=1.5$ km³. Size check: the ice fills well under the 3 km³ box (10 km × 1 km × 0.3 km) around it."],"id":"w03-p3-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the shadow of the ice on the yz-plane, between the floor z = y^2 and the top z = 1, with one point (y, z) marked. Right: a side view showing that at height z the ice runs in x from 0 to the surface x = 1 - z.","caption":"Projected onto the $yz$-plane, the ice fills $y^2\\le z\\le1$. Over each point $(y,z)$, $x$ runs from $0$ to the ice surface $x=1-z$.","file":"w03-p3-a"}},{"expected":"10*0.5*4/3","units":"km²","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The shadow is where the floor lies below the ceiling: $y^2\\le1-x$.","In scaled units its area is $\\int_0^1 2\\sqrt{1-x}\\,dx$; multiply by $LW$ to get square kilometers."],"id":"w03-p3-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"The map shadow D in the xy-plane: a region bounded by y = sqrt(1 - x) and y = -sqrt(1 - x), from the upper end x = 0 to the end of the tongue at x = 1, where the floor meets the surface. A vertical strip at one x has width 2 sqrt(1 - x). The axis labels give the scale factors L = 10 km and W = 0.5 km.","caption":"The footprint is where the floor lies below the surface, $y^2\\le1-x$. A strip at fixed $x$ has width $2\\sqrt{1-x}$ in scaled units.","file":"w03-p3-b"}},{"expected":"3/7*300","units":"m","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Each small piece of ice counts its height $z$ times its volume $dV$: the average height is $\\iiint z\\,dV$ divided by the volume, both in scaled units.","Projecting onto the $xy$-plane, the inner integral of $z$ is $\\big((1-x)^2-y^4\\big)/2$. At the end, divide by the scaled volume and multiply by $H=300$ m."],"id":"w03-p3-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the cross-section of the ice at a fixed x, between the floor z = y^2 and the surface z = 1 - x, with a vertical column and one small piece at height z. Right: the map shadow D with the line at that x, where y runs from -c to c, with c = sqrt(1 - x).","caption":"Each small piece counts its height $z$ times its volume. Integrate $z$ up each column, from the floor $z=y^2$ to the surface $z=1-x$, then across the shadow $D$.","file":"w03-p3-c"}}]},{"id":"w03-p4","questions":[{"expected":"(1-exp(-1.47)*(1+1.47))/1.47","units":"","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Reverse the order: for fixed $x$, $p$ runs from $0$ to $x^3$, so the reversed integral is $\\int_0^1 x^3f(x)\\,dx$.","Substitute $u=1.47x^3$; then $\\int ue^{-u}\\,du$ needs integration by parts."],"id":"w03-p4-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Two copies of the region under p = x^3 for x from 0 to 1. Left: a horizontal slice at power level p from x = the cube root of p to x = 1. Right: a vertical slice at scaled speed x from p = 0 up to p = x^3.","caption":"The limits $\\sqrt[3]{p}\\le x\\le1$ describe the region under $p=x^3$. Reversed, $p$ runs from $0$ up to $x^3$ for each $x$.","file":"w03-p4-a"}},{"expected":"x^2/2*exp(-1.47*x^3)","units":"","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["At fixed $x$, the chain leaves $0\\le z\\le y\\le x$: a triangle in the $yz$-plane.","The integrand does not depend on $y$ or $z$, so the inner double integral is (area of that triangle) × $e^{-1.47x^3}$."],"id":"w03-p4-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the solid 0 <= z <= y <= x <= 1, a tetrahedron with corners (0,0,0), (1,0,0), (1,1,0), and (1,1,1), with the slice at one fixed x shaded. Right: that slice in the yz-plane, a right triangle with y from 0 to x and z from 0 up to y.","caption":"Fixing $x$ first cuts the solid in a triangle, $0\\le z\\le y\\le x$. The integrand does not depend on $y$ or $z$.","file":"w03-p4-b"}},{"expected":"310*8.76*(1-exp(-1.47))/1.47","units":"GWh","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Substitute $u=1.47x^3$ in your reordered integral to find the capacity factor.","Annual energy = capacity factor × 310 MW × 8760 h, and 1 GWh = 1000 MWh. Size check: running flat out all year would give about 2,700 GWh."],"id":"w03-p4-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: arrows from points of the interval 0 <= x <= 1 to the interval 0 <= u <= 1.47, under u = 1.47x^3 and du = 4.41x^2 dx. Right: a box for full power all year, 310 MW times 8760 h; the farm energy is the capacity factor times this.","caption":"The substitution $u=1.47x^3$ changes the limits to $0\\le u\\le1.47$. The annual energy is then the capacity factor times 310 MW × 8760 h.","file":"w03-p4-c"}}]},{"id":"w03-p5","questions":[{"expected":"120*18*3.5","units":"t","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The water fills a box 120 m × 18 m × 3.5 m.","Volume in m³ equals mass in tonnes. Size check: the water alone outweighs the largest ship."],"id":"w03-p5-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Oblique drawing of the water in the basin as a box, 0 to 120 m in x, 0 to 18 m in y, and 0 to 3.5 m in z, with one small box dV inside. The depth is drawn four times too tall.","caption":"The water is a box, so $\\iiint1\\,dV$ is length × width × depth. Each cubic meter has a mass of 1 tonne.","file":"w03-p5-a"}},{"expected":"(120*18*3.5+3000)*1000*9.81*113/(3.6*10^9)","units":"MWh","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Work = (total mass) × $g$ × height, with $g=9.81$ m/s².","Convert tonnes to kilograms, and use 1 MWh $=3.6\\times10^9$ J."],"id":"w03-p5-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"The basin with a ship, lifted from its start position through h = 113 m, with the notes: mass m = water from part (a) plus the 3,000 t ship; work = m g h with g = 9.81 m/s^2; 1 t = 1000 kg; 1 MWh = 3.6 x 10^9 J.","caption":"Lifting a mass $m$ through a height $h$ takes work $mgh$. Convert tonnes to kilograms first and joules to megawatt-hours last.","file":"w03-p5-b"}},{"expected":"0.2*pi/100*1000*(pi^2-4)/(2*pi^3)","units":"m","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Find $j_0$ from the full speed: integrating the jerk twice gives a speed of $j_0T^2/\\pi$ at time $T$.","Use your reordered formula. With $w=u/T$, $\\int_0^1(1-w)^2\\sin(\\pi w)\\,dw=(\\pi^2-4)/\\pi^3$. Size check: the answer is less than 2 m, the distance at full speed for all 10 s."],"id":"w03-p5-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: for a fixed u, the triangle u <= s <= t <= T in the st-plane, with both legs of length T - u. Right: the jerk j0 sin(pi u/T) and the weight (T - u)^2/2 plotted against u from 0 to 10 s, each scaled to its maximum.","caption":"For fixed $u$, the pairs $(s,t)$ fill a triangle with legs $T-u$, which gives the weight $(T-u)^2/2$. The weight is largest early, while the jerk is still small.","file":"w03-p5-c"}}]}]},{"week":4,"problems":[{"id":"w04-p1","questions":[{"expected":"1","units":"","kind":"choice","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Look for two different inputs with the same output. What point does $r=0$ give, whatever $\\theta$ is?","Compare $T(r,0)$ with $T(r,2\\pi)$."],"options":["Only the inputs with $r=0$.","All inputs with $r=0$ (they all give the pivot), and each pair $(r,0)$, $(r,2\\pi)$ (the same spoke).","The inputs with $\\theta=\\pi$, which land on the negative $x$-axis.","None: polar coordinates are one-to-one on this rectangle."],"id":"w04-p1-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the parameter rectangle D-star in the r-theta plane, r from 0 to L and theta from 0 to 2 pi, with its four edges in four colors: r = 0, r = L, theta = 0, and theta = 2 pi. A dashed line r = 800 and a dotted line theta = pi/2 cross it. Right: the circular field, where the dashed line has become a dashed circle of radius 800, traced counterclockwise as theta grows, and the dotted line has become the spoke straight up from the pivot.","caption":"Lines $r=$ const become circles and lines $\\theta=$ const become spokes. Follow each colored edge of $D^*$ the same way, and look for edges that land on the same points.","file":"w04-p1-a"}},{"expected":"650*20*pi*r/(128*43560)","units":"gpm","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The ring is the image of $[r-5,r+5]\\times[0,2\\pi]$ under the polar map, so its area is $\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\!\\int_{r-5}^{r+5}\\rho\\,d\\rho\\,d\\theta$.","The ring area is $20\\pi r$ ft², and the $128\\times43{,}560$ ft² field shares 650 gpm. Size check: a nozzle near the far end, $r\\approx1300$ ft, should need roughly 10 gpm."],"id":"w04-p1-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the rho-theta plane with a thin vertical strip from rho = r - 5 to r + 5 (drawn wider than to scale; its width is 10 ft) running from theta = 0 to 2 pi, with one small cell d rho d theta shaded darker. Right: the field with the matching ring around the pivot at distance r, and one small piece of the ring shaded and labeled dA = rho d rho d theta.","caption":"The polar map wraps the strip $r-5\\le\\rho\\le r+5$, $0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$ around the pivot into the ring; each small piece of the ring has area $\\rho\\,d\\rho\\,d\\theta$, not $d\\rho\\,d\\theta$.","file":"w04-p1-b"}},{"expected":"3/4","units":"","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Flow is proportional to the area watered, so compare areas.","Compare $\\int_{L/2}^{L}r\\,dr$ with $\\int_0^L r\\,dr$."],"id":"w04-p1-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the field split into an inner disk, watered by the inner half of the pipe, and an outer ring, watered by the outer half. Right: the graph of the weight r against r from 0 to L, a straight line through the origin; the area under it from 0 to L/2 is shaded and labeled as the integral of r dr from 0 to L/2, and the area from L/2 to L is shaded in another color and labeled as the integral of r dr from L/2 to L.","caption":"Each half of the pipe gets water in proportion to the area it waters, $\\int r\\,dr$ over its half: compare the two shaded areas under the line.","file":"w04-p1-c"}}]},{"id":"w04-p2","questions":[{"expected":"3*sqrt(3)/2*0.142^2","units":"nm²","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["$T$ is linear, so the cell area is $|\\det A|$ times the area of the unit square.","With $a=\\sqrt3\\times0.142$ nm, $\\det A=\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}a^2$. Size check: expect a few hundredths of a nm²."],"id":"w04-p2-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the unit square in the uv-plane. Right: its image under T, a rhombus spanned by the arrows a1 = (a, 0) and a2 = (a/2, root 3 over 2 times a), with all four sides of length a, a 60 degree angle at the origin, and a dashed vertical height labeled root 3 over 2 times a.","caption":"$T$ sends the unit square (area 1) onto the rhombus spanned by $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$; its area is $|\\det A|$, which is also its base $a$ times its height.","file":"w04-p2-a"}},{"expected":["x-y/sqrt(3)","2*y/sqrt(3)"],"units":"","kind":"vector","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["With $a=1$, $T$ says $x=u+v/2$ and $y=\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}v$. Undo it: solve for $u$ and $v$.","Find $v$ from the second equation first, then use $x=u+v/2$ to find $u$."],"id":"w04-p2-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"The xy-plane with a = 1, covered by a slanted grid of lines u = const and horizontal lines v = const. From the origin an arrow u a1 runs along the x-axis, and from its tip an arrow v a2 slants up to a point (x, y). A dotted vertical segment from the point down to the x-axis is labeled y = root 3 over 2 times v; a dashed horizontal line through the point is labeled v = const, and a dashed slanted line through it is labeled u = const.","caption":"Every point is $u\\,\\mathbf a_1+v\\,\\mathbf a_2$: read $v$ from the height $y=\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}v$, then take away the sideways shift $v/2$ of the step $v\\,\\mathbf a_2$ to find $u$.","file":"w04-p2-b"}},{"expected":"2","units":"","kind":"choice","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["For each map, try to solve for $(u,v)$ from $(x,y)$. A map fails if some $(x,y)$ has two different solutions.","For $S$, add and subtract the two equations; for $F$, solve $x=u^2$ for $u$; for $G$, start from $u=x$."],"options":["$T(u,v)=(u+\\tfrac12v,\\ \\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}v)$, the lattice map","$S(u,v)=(u+v,\\ u-v)$","$F(u,v)=(u^2,\\ v)$","$G(u,v)=(u,\\ v+u^3)$"],"id":"w04-p2-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: a square grid of lines u = const and v = const in the uv-plane, from -1 to 1. Right: its image under the lattice map T: the u-lines become parallel slanted lines and the v-lines become parallel horizontal lines, evenly spaced, with no two lines of the same family meeting.","caption":"The lattice map sends the grid to a slanted grid that never folds over itself, so each output comes from exactly one input. Ask the same of $S$, $F$, and $G$: can two different inputs give the same output?","file":"w04-p2-c"}}]},{"id":"w04-p3","questions":[{"expected":"4*(u^2+v^2)","units":"","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The Jacobian is the determinant $x_uy_v-x_vy_u$.","$x_u=2u$, $x_v=-2v$, $y_u=2v$, $y_v=2u$."],"id":"w04-p3-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the uv-plane with two small squares of the same size, one near (0.3, 0.3) and one near (0.8, 0.6). Right: their images under T in the xy-plane, two curved patches of different sizes, the one from farther out much larger. From one corner of the larger patch two arrows show its sides, labeled (x_u, y_u) delta u and (x_v, y_v) delta v.","caption":"A small square of area $\\Delta u\\,\\Delta v$ becomes a near-parallelogram with sides $(x_u,y_u)\\,\\Delta u$ and $(x_v,y_v)\\,\\Delta v$, so its area is $|x_uy_v-x_vy_u|\\,\\Delta u\\,\\Delta v$.","file":"w04-p3-a"}},{"expected":"8/3","units":"AU²","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["$T$ is one-to-one on the square, so the area is $\\int_0^1\\!\\int_0^1 J\\,du\\,dv$, with $J$ from (a).","Split $4(u^2+v^2)$ into $4u^2+4v^2$; by symmetry the two terms give the same integral over the square."],"id":"w04-p3-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the unit square with its four edges colored: v = 0 along the bottom, u = 1 on the right, v = 1 on top, and u = 0 on the left. Right: the shaded image region in the xy-plane, in AU, bounded below by the x-axis from -1 to 1 (v = 0 gives the right half, u = 0 the left half) and above by two parabolic arcs, the images of u = 1 and v = 1, which meet at (0, 2).","caption":"$T$ is one-to-one on the square, so the area of the shaded image is $\\int_0^1\\!\\int_0^1 J\\,du\\,dv$ with $J$ from (a); you never need the curved edges.","file":"w04-p3-b"}},{"expected":"0.2947*sqrt(0.7053/0.2947)*(2*0.2947+1)/3/(pi*sqrt(2*0.2947))*365.25","units":"days","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The area of $D$ is $\\iint_{D^*}4(u^2+v^2)\\,du\\,dv$, where $D^*$ is the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(\\sqrt q,0)$, $(\\sqrt q,\\sqrt{1-q})$. Size check: the area is a few tenths of an AU² and the sweep rate a few AU² per year, so expect weeks, not months.","Integrate with $v$ first, from $0$ to $ku$, where $k=\\sqrt{(1-q)/q}$. Then divide by the sweep rate $\\pi\\sqrt{2q}$ AU² per year and convert to days."],"decimals":1,"id":"w04-p3-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the triangle D-star in the uv-plane with vertices (0, 0), (root q, 0), and (root q, root(1 - q)); its bottom edge v = 0, right edge u = root q, and slanted edge v = k u are colored, and a dashed vertical slice at one u runs from v = 0 up to v = k u. Right: its image D in the xy-plane: the bottom edge becomes the segment from the Sun to perihelion, the right edge becomes the orbit arc up to the point 1 AU from the Sun, the slanted edge becomes the segment from the Sun to that point, and the dashed slice becomes a dashed arc inside D.","caption":"The area of $D$ is $\\iint_{D^*}4(u^2+v^2)\\,du\\,dv$ over the triangle $D^*$: on each vertical slice $v$ runs from $0$ to $ku$, and $u$ runs from $0$ to $\\sqrt q$.","file":"w04-p3-c"}}]},{"id":"w04-p4","questions":[{"expected":"-2*pi/u","units":"","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Write $R=\\sqrt{-2\\ln u}$, so $x=R\\cos2\\pi v$ and $y=R\\sin2\\pi v$, and use $dR/du=-1/(uR)$.","$x_u=-\\cos(2\\pi v)/(uR)$ and $x_v=-2\\pi R\\sin2\\pi v$. In $x_uy_v-x_vy_u$, the $R$ cancels and $\\cos^2+\\sin^2=1$."],"id":"w04-p4-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the unit square of inputs with a small rectangle near u = 0.3, v = 0.06, and two arrows from its corner: increasing u points right and increasing v points up. Right: the image of the rectangle in the xy-plane, a small piece of a ring at radius R = root(-2 ln u). The image of the increasing-u arrow points inward toward the origin, and the image of the increasing-v arrow points counterclockwise along the circle.","caption":"Follow the $u$- and $v$-directions through $T$: their images are the columns $(x_u,y_u)$ and $(x_v,y_v)$ of the Jacobian matrix, and whether the turn from the first to the second is counterclockwise or clockwise decides the sign.","file":"w04-p4-a"}},{"expected":"exp(-(x^2+y^2)/2)/(2*pi)","units":"","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The inverse Jacobian is the reciprocal of the forward Jacobian from (a); take its absolute value.","Then write $u$ in terms of $x$ and $y$, using $x^2+y^2=-2\\ln u$."],"id":"w04-p4-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the unit square of inputs with a thin shaded rectangle, labeled: preimage, probability equals its area. Right: the xy-plane with a small shaded patch of area delta A, a piece of a ring between two circles about the origin; a note says x squared plus y squared equals -2 ln u on each circle.","caption":"The chance that a point lands in the patch $\\Delta A$ is the area of its preimage, about $|\\partial(u,v)/\\partial(x,y)|\\,\\Delta A$; on the circle through the patch, $x^2+y^2=-2\\ln u$.","file":"w04-p4-b"}},{"expected":"1-exp(-2)","units":"","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The probability is the area of the preimage of the disk. Which $u$ give a radius $\\sqrt{-2\\ln u}\\le2$? Size check: most of the sample points in the figure lie inside radius 2.","Or integrate the density from (b) in polar coordinates, from $r=0$ to $r=2$."],"id":"w04-p4-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the unit square of inputs with a vertical strip shaded, for all v, from a line at small u, marked as the line where root(-2 ln u) = 2, over to u = 1. Right: the shaded disk of radius 2 about the origin.","caption":"The disk of radius 2 is the image of a strip: all $v$, and the $u$ with $\\sqrt{-2\\ln u}\\le2$. Its probability is the area of that strip.","file":"w04-p4-c"}}]},{"id":"w04-p5","questions":[{"expected":"1/cos(phi)","units":"","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["$x=\\lambda$ does not depend on $\\phi$, and $y$ does not depend on $\\lambda$, so the Jacobian is $y_\\phi$.","Differentiate $\\ln\\tan(\\pi/4+\\phi/2)$ with the chain rule, then use $2\\sin A\\cos A=\\sin2A$."],"id":"w04-p5-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the longitude-latitude plane with two equal small squares, delta lambda by delta phi, one at latitudes 0 to 10 degrees and one at 60 to 70 degrees. Right: their images on the Mercator map: both keep the width delta lambda, but the one from 60 to 70 degrees is more than twice as tall; each height is labeled approximately y_phi delta phi.","caption":"$x=\\lambda$ keeps every width, so only heights change: the Jacobian is $y_\\phi$, the rate at which map height grows with latitude.","file":"w04-p5-a"}},{"expected":"1/cos(72*pi/180)^2","units":"","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Divide the Jacobian by the sphere’s area factor $\\cos\\phi$.","The ratio is $\\sec^2\\phi$; evaluate it at $\\phi=72^\\circ$. Size check: $\\cos72^\\circ$ is about 0.3, so expect a factor near 10."],"id":"w04-p5-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: a globe with parallels and meridians and a small patch near latitude 72 degrees north shaded, labeled area approximately cos phi delta lambda delta phi. Right: the same patch on the Mercator map, a rectangle of width delta lambda and height J delta phi, labeled area approximately J delta lambda delta phi.","caption":"The areal scale factor is (map area)/(true area) for the same small patch: divide $J\\,\\Delta\\lambda\\,\\Delta\\phi$ by $\\cos\\phi\\,\\Delta\\lambda\\,\\Delta\\phi$, then set $\\phi=72^\\circ$.","file":"w04-p5-b"}},{"expected":"2166086/cos(72*pi/180)^2/2344858","units":"","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Multiply each true area by its areal scale factor, then divide.","At the equator the factor is 1."],"id":"w04-p5-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Two squares drawn to scale and almost the same size: Greenland, 2,166,086 square kilometers, and DR Congo, 2,344,858 square kilometers. Under Greenland: times the areal scale factor at 72 degrees north. Under DR Congo: times the areal scale factor at the equator.","caption":"On the map, each true area is multiplied by the areal scale factor at its own latitude; compare the two products.","file":"w04-p5-c"}}]}]},{"week":5,"problems":[{"id":"w05-p1","questions":[{"expected":"2*h/5","units":"ft","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["A thin vertical strip at $x$ has area $h(1-x^2/w^2)\\,dx$ and balances halfway up, so it adds $\\tfrac12h^2(1-x^2/w^2)^2\\,dx$ to the moment $M_x$.","The area is $M=\\tfrac43hw$. Integrate the strip moments from $x=-w$ to $x=w$ to get $M_x$, then $\\bar y=M_x/M$."],"id":"w05-p1-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"A single parabolic region under y = h(1 - x squared over w squared), from x = -w to x = w, with height h marked. One thin vertical strip at position x, of width dx, is highlighted; its height is h(1 - x squared over w squared), and a dot halfway up the strip marks where the strip balances.","caption":"One thin strip at $x$: its area is its height times $dx$, and it balances halfway up, so it adds (area)$\\times$(half its height) to $M_x$.","file":"w05-p1-a"}},{"expected":"2/5*(630^2*315-613^2*261)/(630*315-613*261)","units":"ft","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Subtract the inner region’s mass and moment from the outer region’s, then divide. Size check: the outer region alone balances at $\\tfrac25\\cdot630=252$ ft, and removing the inner region, which balances lower, should raise that.","Each region has mass $\\tfrac43hw$ and moment $\\tfrac8{15}h^2w$; use $h=630$, $w=315$ (outer) and $h=613$, $w=261$ (inner)."],"id":"w05-p1-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Three small drawings in a row: the outer parabolic region with h = 630 and w = 315, a minus sign, the inner parabolic region with h = 613 and w = 261, an equals sign, and the arch band A that is left over.","caption":"The band $A$ is the outer region with the inner region cut out, so its mass and its moment $M_x$ are (outer) minus (inner), each from part (a)’s formulas.","file":"w05-p1-b"}},{"expected":"406441825784/1768433009","units":"ft","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Let $I_k=\\iint_A y^k\\,dA$ for $k=0,1,2$. Under $y=h(1-x^2/w^2)$ these integrals are $\\tfrac43wh$, $\\tfrac8{15}wh^2$, $\\tfrac{32}{105}wh^3$; subtract inner from outer.","Then $M=54I_0-\\tfrac{37}{630}I_1$ and $M_x=54I_1-\\tfrac{37}{630}I_2$, so $\\bar y=M_x/M$."],"decimals":1,"id":"w05-p1-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the arch band shaded by density, darkest at the ground and lightest at the top. Right: a graph with height y up the side and density across, a straight line from 54 at the ground to 17 at the top, y = 630 ft.","caption":"The density $\\delta=54-\\tfrac{37}{630}y$ falls in a straight line from 54 at the ground to 17 at the top, so the low parts of $A$ count more in both $M$ and $M_x$.","file":"w05-p1-c"}}]},{"id":"w05-p2","questions":[{"expected":"4*pi*(a/3-b/5)","units":"","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Use spherical coordinates; for a density that depends only on $\\rho$, the two angle integrals together give $4\\pi$.","With $dV=\\rho^2\\sin\\phi\\,d\\rho\\,d\\phi\\,d\\theta$, the radial integral is $\\int_0^1(a-b\\rho^2)\\rho^2\\,d\\rho$."],"id":"w05-p2-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: a cross-section of the ball with one thin ring-shaped shell at radius rho highlighted, labeled thickness d rho. Right: a graph of the density a - b rho squared against rho from 0 to 1, starting at a and falling to a - b at the surface.","caption":"Cut the ball into thin shells: every point of the shell at radius $\\rho$ has the same density $a-b\\rho^2$, so only one $\\rho$-integral is left after the angles.","file":"w05-p2-a"}},{"expected":"2/3*(a/5-b/7)/(a/3-b/5)","units":"","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The distance to the spin axis is $\\rho\\sin\\phi$, so $I=\\iiint(a-b\\rho^2)(\\rho\\sin\\phi)^2\\rho^2\\sin\\phi\\,d\\rho\\,d\\phi\\,d\\theta$.","Use $\\int_0^\\pi\\sin^3\\phi\\,d\\phi=\\tfrac43$, then divide $I$ by the mass from part (a)."],"id":"w05-p2-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"A cross-section of the ball through the z-axis. A point at distance rho from the center, at angle phi from the z-axis, is joined to the axis by a horizontal segment labeled rho sin phi, and a dashed circle shows the path the point traces around the axis. Notes say each bit of mass adds delta times (rho sin phi) squared times dV to I.","caption":"The moment of inertia weights each bit of mass by its squared distance to the spin axis, $(\\rho\\sin\\phi)^2$, not by $\\rho^2$.","file":"w05-p2-b"}},{"expected":"5.513/(1-3*0.79326608/5)","units":"g/cm³","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The factor depends only on $k=b/a$: solve $\\tfrac23(\\tfrac15-\\tfrac k7)\\big/(\\tfrac13-\\tfrac k5)=0.3308$ for $k$.","The mean density is $M/V=3(\\tfrac a3-\\tfrac b5)=a(1-\\tfrac35k)$. Size check: $a$ is in g/cm$^3$ and must be well above the mean, $5.513$."],"decimals":1,"id":"w05-p2-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"A graph of the factor I over M R squared against k = b over a, from 0 to 1. The curve starts at 0.4 for a uniform ball and falls as k grows. A dashed horizontal line marks the measured 0.3308, and the crossing point is circled with the note: solve for this k.","caption":"The factor depends only on $k=b/a$: find where the curve meets the measured $0.3308$, then use the mean density to get $a$.","file":"w05-p2-c"}}]},{"id":"w05-p3","questions":[{"expected":"pi*h^3/3","units":"km³","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["With a $45^\\circ$ top tilt the blind region is $r\\le z$, so at height $z$ it is a disk of radius $z$.","Integrate $r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\,dz$ over $0\\le r\\le z$, $0\\le z\\le h$, $0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$."],"id":"w05-p3-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Side view of the blind cone, a V shape opening upward from the radar, with 45 degree sides, cut off at height h. A horizontal slice at height z is drawn as a disk whose radius equals z.","caption":"With a $45^\\circ$ top tilt, the horizontal slice at height $z$ is a disk $0\\le r\\le z$; stack the slices from $z=0$ to $z=h$.","file":"w05-p3-a"}},{"expected":"3*h/4","units":"km","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Slice the region horizontally: the slice at height $z$ is a disk of area $\\pi z^2$.","The moment is $\\int_0^h z\\cdot\\pi z^2\\,dz$; divide it by the volume from part (a)."],"id":"w05-p3-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the blind cone with horizontal slices at four heights, each wider than the one below, and a dashed line at h/2. Right: a graph of slice area pi z squared against height z from 0 to h, rising steeply, with h/2 marked.","caption":"The slice area $\\pi z^2$ grows with height, so the upper half of the cone holds much more volume than the lower half. Weight each height $z$ by its slice area.","file":"w05-p3-b"}},{"expected":"1-sqrt(2)/2","units":"","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["In spherical coordinates the blind region is $0\\le\\rho\\le100$, $0\\le\\phi\\le\\pi/4$, $0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$.","The half-ball has the same $\\rho$ and $\\theta$ limits but $0\\le\\phi\\le\\pi/2$, so only the $\\phi$ integrals differ. Size check: the fraction should be less than one half; can you see why from your sketch?"],"id":"w05-p3-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: side view of the half-ball of radius 100 km above the radar, with the blind cone between phi = 0 and phi = pi/4 shaded and the ground at phi = pi/2. Right: a graph of sin phi from 0 to pi/2, with the part from 0 to pi/4 shaded for the blind cone and the rest lightly shaded.","caption":"Both regions have $0\\le\\rho\\le100$ and $0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$; only the $\\phi$-limits differ. The factor $\\sin\\phi$ in $dV$ is small near the axis, so the blind cone holds less than its angle suggests.","file":"w05-p3-c"}}]},{"id":"w05-p4","questions":[{"expected":"pi*(1-exp(-r^2))","units":"","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Use polar coordinates, calling the polar radius $\\rho$ because $r$ is the aperture radius: $\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^r e^{-\\rho^2}\\rho\\,d\\rho\\,d\\theta$.","Substitute $w=\\rho^2$. Check: as $r$ grows, your answer should level off at a finite total."],"id":"w05-p4-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the star image, brightest at the center and fading outward, with a circular aperture of radius r and one thin ring at radius rho highlighted. Right: a graph of the ring light, e to the minus rho squared times 2 pi rho, against rho, with the area from 0 up to the aperture edge r shaded.","caption":"Add up thin rings: the ring at $\\rho$ has area about $2\\pi\\rho\\,d\\rho$ and brightness $e^{-\\rho^2}$. The light inside the aperture is the shaded area.","file":"w05-p4-a"}},{"expected":"pi/(b-1)","units":"","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["In polar coordinates the integrand times the area factor $r$ is $r^{1-2b}$.","Integrate from $r=1$ to $r=T$, then let $T\\to\\infty$."],"id":"w05-p4-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: lights spread over the plane outside a white dark circle of radius 1, with one thin ring at radius r highlighted and a dashed circle at radius T. Right: a graph of r to the power 1 - 2b, drawn for b = 1.5, falling from r = 1, with the area from 1 to T shaded.","caption":"In polar coordinates each ring at radius $r$ adds $r^{-2b}\\cdot2\\pi r\\,dr$. Integrate from $1$ to $T$, then let $T\\to\\infty$.","file":"w05-p4-b"}},{"expected":"1/2","units":"","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Redo part (b) with inner radius $a$: the integral is proportional to $a^{2-2b}$.","Here $2-2b=-\\tfrac12$. Check: the ratio is a pure number less than 1, because a wider dark zone removes the nearest lights."],"id":"w05-p4-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: dots for city lights spread evenly around the observatory, with circles at 10 km and 40 km; the dots between the two circles are orange, marking the lights removed when the dark zone grows. Right: a graph of ring glow, proportional to r to the minus 1.5, against ring radius in km, with the area from 10 km on shaded light and the area from 40 km on shaded orange; both tails continue past the edge.","caption":"Growing the dark zone removes only the lights between 10 and 40 km; all the lights beyond 40 km still shine. Compare the two tails using part (b) with inner radius $a$.","file":"w05-p4-c"}}]},{"id":"w05-p5","questions":[{"expected":"(3*660000/(4*pi*7850))^(1/3)","units":"m","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["$V=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi\\int_0^R\\rho^2\\sin\\phi\\,d\\rho\\,d\\phi\\,d\\theta=\\tfrac43\\pi R^3$.","Set $7850\\,V=660{,}000$ kg and solve for $R$. Size check: $R$ should be a few meters."],"id":"w05-p5-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Two views of the ball. Side view: the z-axis points up, a radius rho is drawn, and an arc shows phi sweeping from 0 at the top to pi at the bottom. Top view: a circle with an arc showing theta sweeping all the way around, from 0 to 2 pi.","caption":"To cover the whole ball, $\\rho$ runs from 0 to $R$, $\\phi$ from 0 (straight up) to $\\pi$ (straight down), and $\\theta$ once around, from 0 to $2\\pi$.","file":"w05-p5-a"}},{"expected":"2/5*660*(3*660000/(4*pi*7850))^(2/3)","units":"t·m²","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The squared distance to the vertical axis is $x^2+y^2=\\rho^2\\sin^2\\phi$.","You should find $I=\\tfrac25MR^2$; use $M=660$ t and $R$ from part (a)."],"id":"w05-p5-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"A cross-section of the ball through the vertical z-axis, shaded orange by squared distance from the axis, darkest at the left and right edges. A point at distance rho and angle phi is joined to the axis by a segment labeled rho sin phi. Notes give the squared distance x squared plus y squared equals (rho sin phi) squared, and the integral for I.","caption":"The steel farthest from the vertical axis counts most: each bit adds $\\delta\\,(\\rho\\sin\\phi)^2\\,dV$, which is where the extra $\\sin^2\\phi$ comes from.","file":"w05-p5-b"}},{"expected":"3/8*(3*660000/(4*pi*7850))^(1/3)","units":"m","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Use $\\pi/2\\le\\phi\\le\\pi$; the depth below the center is $-z=-\\rho\\cos\\phi$.","The $\\phi$-integral $\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}(-\\cos\\phi)\\sin\\phi\\,d\\phi$ equals $\\tfrac12$. Divide the depth moment by the half-ball volume $\\tfrac23\\pi R^3$."],"id":"w05-p5-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"A cross-section of the ball with its lower half shaded. A point in the lower half, at distance rho from the center and angle phi from the upward z-axis, has a vertical segment from its level up to z = 0 labeled depth minus rho cos phi. Notes give the limits pi/2 to pi for phi and the depth moment integral.","caption":"In the lower half $\\cos\\phi\\le0$, so the depth $-\\rho\\cos\\phi$ is positive. Integrate it over the half-ball, then divide by the half-ball volume.","file":"w05-p5-c"}}]}]},{"week":6,"problems":[{"id":"w06-p1","questions":[{"expected":"sqrt(1+(9*t/12800)^2)","units":"","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The speed is the length of the velocity vector $\\mathbf c^{\\prime}(t)$.","Differentiate: $\\mathbf c^{\\prime}(t)=(1,\\,9t/12800)$. Then take its length."],"id":"w06-p1-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the parabolic cable with two velocity arrows, a horizontal one at the lowest point and one tilted up along the cable at t = 480. Right: a right triangle with horizontal leg 1, vertical leg 9t/12800, and the speed as its hypotenuse","caption":"The velocity $\\mathbf c^{\\prime}(t)$ always moves 1 m to the right per unit of $t$ and rises by $9t/12800$, so the speed is the hypotenuse of the triangle on the right.","file":"w06-p1-a"}},{"expected":"1321.97431941635","units":"m","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Each short piece of cable has length $ds=\\|\\mathbf c^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,dt$, so $L$ is the integral of the speed from part (a), from $t=-640$ to $t=640$.","Substituting $t=k\\sinh u$ turns the integrand into $k\\cosh^2u\\,du$; a calculator’s numerical integral also works. Your answer is in meters and must be more than 1,280."],"id":"w06-p1-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Graph of the speed against t from -640 to 640: a curve that equals 1 at t = 0 and rises slightly toward both ends; the area under it is shaded, split into a 1 by 1,280 rectangle and a thin orange sliver on top that is thickest near the towers","caption":"$L$ is the whole shaded area under the speed graph: the $1\\times1280$ m rectangle plus a thin sliver, thickest near the towers, where the cable is steepest.","file":"w06-p1-b"}},{"expected":"49.2045532645424","units":"m","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Compute $\\int_C y\\,ds$ along the cable and divide by the length $L$ from part (b).","Along $\\Psi$, $y\\,ds=\\tfrac{k^2}{2}\\sinh^2u\\cosh^2u\\,du=\\tfrac{k^2}{8}\\sinh^2(2u)\\,du$. Expect a little more than $h/3=48$ m."],"id":"w06-p1-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"The parabolic cable with 31 dots spaced at equal lengths along it, crowding slightly toward the towers, and a dashed horizontal line at the average height over x, h/3 = 48 m","caption":"Each dot marks an equal length, and so an equal weight, of cable; $\\bar y=\\int_C y\\,ds/L$ is the average height of the dots, and their crowding near the towers lifts it a little above $h/3=48$ m.","file":"w06-p1-c"}}]},{"id":"w06-p2","questions":[{"expected":"6371*pi","units":"km","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Parametrize the circle as $\\mathbf c(t)=(\\rho\\cos t,\\rho\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$, and integrate the speed.","The speed $\\|\\mathbf c^{\\prime}(t)\\|$ is constant, equal to $\\rho=3185.5$ km. Since $\\cos60^\\circ=1/2$, expect about half the length of the equator, $2\\pi R\\approx40{,}000$ km."],"id":"w06-p2-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: a cut through Earth along its axis, with radius R drawn to the 60 degree south latitude and the horizontal distance rho from the axis to that point. Right: the voyage circle seen from above the South Pole, with a short highlighted arc ds subtending a small angle dt and an arrow showing counterclockwise (westward) travel","caption":"Left: the $60^\\circ$ S circle has radius $\\rho=R\\cos60^\\circ$. Right: on $\\mathbf c(t)=(\\rho\\cos t,\\rho\\sin t)$, a small step $dt$ covers the arc $ds=\\rho\\,dt$.","file":"w06-p2-a"}},{"expected":"-1.8*6371*pi","units":"km²/h","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["On a short piece of the voyage, $\\mathbf u\\cdot d\\mathbf r$ is the current’s signed speed along the direction of travel times the distance sailed. The drift is perpendicular to $\\mathbf c^{\\prime}(t)$, so it gives zero.","Integrate $\\mathbf u(\\mathbf c(t))\\cdot\\mathbf c^{\\prime}(t)$ from $0$ to $2\\pi$. The eastward part gives the constant $\\frac{1.8}{\\rho}(\\rho\\sin t,-\\rho\\cos t)\\cdot(-\\rho\\sin t,\\rho\\cos t)$. Expect a negative answer: the ship fights the current."],"id":"w06-p2-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the voyage circle with black travel arrows pointing counterclockwise outside it, teal current arrows pointing clockwise inside it, and minus signs all around. Right: at one point, the travel direction points up, the eastward part of the current points down, and the drift points sideways at a right angle","caption":"Going west (counterclockwise on the map), $\\mathbf c^{\\prime}(t)$ points against the eastward part of $\\mathbf u$ at every point of $C$, while the drift is perpendicular to $\\mathbf c^{\\prime}(t)$.","file":"w06-p2-b"}},{"expected":"1.8*sqrt(1.01)*6371*pi","units":"km²/h","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["On the circle $\\|\\mathbf u\\|$ is constant: the eastward part and the drift are perpendicular, with sizes $1.8$ and $0.18$ km/h.","Multiply $\\|\\mathbf u\\|$ by the length from part (a). This scalar integral takes no sign from the direction of travel."],"id":"w06-p2-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the voyage circle with equal-length current arrows at eight points, each tilted slightly outward. Right: a right triangle with a long leg of 1.8 km/h along the circle, a short leg of 0.18 km/h outward (drawn 2.5 times longer), and the size of u as the hypotenuse","caption":"At every point of $C$ the current is the same right triangle: $1.8$ km/h along $C$ and $0.18$ km/h straight out, with $\\|\\mathbf u\\|$ as its hypotenuse, which never takes a sign.","file":"w06-p2-c"}}]},{"id":"w06-p3","questions":[{"expected":"1","units":"L·atm","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Parametrize the segment from A to B and substitute into $y\\,dx$.","With $x=1+1.5t$, $y=1-\\tfrac23t$, $0\\le t\\le1$, you get $dx=1.5\\,dt$. Check with the area of the trapezoid under the segment."],"id":"w06-p3-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Pressure-volume diagram with the straight path 1 from A at (1, 1) down to B at (2.5, 1/3), points marked at t = 0, 0.5, and 1, and the trapezoid under the segment down to zero pressure shaded, 1.5 liters wide","caption":"Along path 1, $x$ increases the whole way, so $W=\\int_C y\\,dx$ is the area of the shaded trapezoid under the segment.","file":"w06-p3-a"}},{"expected":"log(3)-1/6","units":"L·atm","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Integrate over each of the two pieces of path 2 and add.","On the isotherm $y=1/x$, so the first piece is $\\int_1^3dx/x$. On the constant-pressure piece $x$ decreases from $3$ to $2.5$, so that piece is negative. Your total should be positive and less than $\\ln3\\approx1.10$."],"id":"w06-p3-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Pressure-volume diagram of path 2: the area under the isotherm y = 1/x from x = 1 to x = 3 is shaded and marked plus; the strip under y = 1/3 from x = 2.5 to x = 3 is hatched and marked minus, because the path runs leftward there","caption":"Path 2 gives two signed areas: the area under the isotherm $y=1/x$ from $x=1$ to $x=3$ counts $+$, and the hatched strip counts $-$, because there $x$ decreases from $3$ to $2.5$.","file":"w06-p3-b"}},{"expected":"5*x*y/2","units":"L·atm","kind":"potential","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Integrate $\\tfrac52y$ with respect to $x$ and add an unknown function $g(y)$.","Differentiate your result with respect to $y$ and compare with $\\tfrac52x$ to show $g$ is constant."],"id":"w06-p3-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Gray arrows of the energy field on the pressure-volume diagram, pointing mostly upward and growing to the right, and an orange horizontal line at a fixed pressure labeled: on this line y is fixed, integrate five halves y in x, then add g(y)","caption":"On a line where $y$ is fixed, $\\partial f/\\partial x=\\tfrac52y$ is a constant, so integrate it in $x$; then fix the unknown $g(y)$ by comparing $\\partial f/\\partial y$ with $\\tfrac52x$.","file":"w06-p3-c"}}]},{"id":"w06-p4","questions":[{"expected":"c/sqrt(x^2+y^2+z^2)","units":"J","kind":"potential","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Integrate $F_1$ with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants; substitute $w=x^2+y^2+z^2$.","Check that the derivative of your answer with respect to $z$ is $-cz/(x^2+y^2+z^2)^{3/2}$."],"id":"w06-p4-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: a horizontal orange line passing above Earth’s center, with gravity arrows at five points on it all pointing toward the center. Right: the graph of the x-component F1 along that line, positive to the left of the center, zero above it, and negative to the right","caption":"Holding $y$ and $z$ fixed means moving along a line parallel to the $x$-axis; there $f$ changes at the rate $F_1$, which is positive before the line passes the center and negative after.","file":"w06-p4-a"}},{"expected":"-1446.27683945079","units":"kJ","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Use the fundamental theorem: the work is $f(\\text{end})-f(\\text{start})$. Only the distances $r=R+h$ from Earth’s center matter.","Expect about $-mg\\,\\Delta h$ (in kJ), but compute with the inverse-square potential; the two differ in the fourth digit."],"id":"w06-p4-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: elevation h(t) of the road model rising almost linearly from 915 m at t = 0 to 2757 m at t = 1 with tiny wiggles. Right: the wiggle term alone, oscillating between -15 m and 15 m and equal to zero at t = 0 and t = 1","caption":"Only $h(0)=915$ m and $h(1)=2757$ m enter $f(\\text{end})-f(\\text{start})$, because $f$ depends only on $r=R+h$; the wiggle term (right) is zero at both ends.","file":"w06-p4-b"}},{"expected":"0","units":"","kind":"choice","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The fundamental theorem gives $f(\\text{end})-f(\\text{start})$. Where does a closed ride end?","The rider’s effort goes into air drag, friction, and body heat, which are not gravity."],"options":["0 J: gravity has a potential everywhere the rider can be, and the ride ends where it starts.","Negative: the rider worked hard, so gravity’s total work must be negative.","It depends on which roads make up the loop.","Positive: descents are faster than climbs."],"id":"w06-p4-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Schematic elevation profile of a closed ride: a climb from Prato to the Stelvio Pass, a descent to Bormio, a climb on another road, and a descent back to Prato at the same height; climbs are orange and marked F dot dr less than zero, descents teal and marked F dot dr greater than zero","caption":"Along a closed ride (schematic), gravity’s work is negative on every climb and positive on every descent, and the ride finishes at Prato, where it started.","file":"w06-p4-c"}}]},{"id":"w06-p5","questions":[{"expected":"12*pi","units":"mT·m","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["On the circle, $\\mathbf B$ is tangent and points counterclockwise, with constant size $6/10$ mT, so on a short arc of length $ds$, $\\mathbf B\\cdot d\\mathbf r=\\tfrac{6}{10}\\,ds$.","Multiply that size by the circle’s length; the answer does not depend on the radius."],"id":"w06-p5-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the dashed circle of radius 10 m around the channel, with equal teal arrows tangent to it pointing counterclockwise and a short highlighted arc ds. Right: graph of the field size 6/r against distance r, with the point 0.6 mT at r = 10 m marked","caption":"On the circle of radius 10 m, $\\mathbf B$ is tangent and points the way you travel, with the same size $6/10$ mT everywhere, so $\\mathbf B\\cdot d\\mathbf r=\\|\\mathbf B\\|\\,ds$.","file":"w06-p5-a"}},{"expected":"3*pi","units":"mT·m","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["On this edge $x=10$ and $dx=0$, so only $B_2\\,dy=60\\,dy/(100+y^2)$ counts.","Use $\\int dy/(100+y^2)=\\tfrac1{10}\\arctan(y/10)$. Size check: $B_2$ falls from $0.6$ mT at the middle of the 20 m edge to $0.3$ mT at its ends."],"id":"w06-p5-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: square S1 with its right edge x = 10 highlighted, field arrows along that edge tilting left at the top and right at the bottom, and an arrow showing upward travel. Right: graph of B2(10, y) = 60/(100 + y^2) from y = -10 to 10, peaking at 0.6 and falling to 0.3 at the ends, with the area under it shaded","caption":"On the right edge $dx=0$, so only $B_2$ counts: the integral is the shaded area under $B_2(10,y)=60/(100+y^2)$ from $y=-10$ to $y=10$.","file":"w06-p5-b"}},{"expected":"6*(y^2-x^2)/(x^2+y^2)^2","units":"mT/m","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Use the quotient rule on $6x/(x^2+y^2)$, treating $y$ as a constant.","Compare with $\\partial B_1/\\partial y$ for $B_1=-6y/(x^2+y^2)$; the two should agree."],"id":"w06-p5-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the horizontal line y = 5 m above the channel, with arrows showing the y-part of B along it, pointing down left of the channel and up to its right. Right: graph of B2 against x with y = 5 held fixed, with a tangent line at one point labeled slope equals the partial derivative of B2 with respect to x","caption":"$\\partial B_2/\\partial x$ is the slope of $B_2$ along a horizontal line with $y$ held fixed, here $y=5$ m; treat $y$ as a constant in the quotient rule.","file":"w06-p5-c"}}]}]},{"week":7,"problems":[{"id":"w07-p1","questions":[{"expected":"6371^2*sin(phi)","units":"km²","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Compute $\\Phi_\\phi$ and $\\Phi_\\theta$, then their dot product and their lengths.","The two tangent vectors are perpendicular, with lengths $R$ and $R\\sin\\phi$."],"id":"w07-p1-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: a sphere with its North Pole, one meridian and one parallel, the angle phi measured from the North Pole, the radius R, and the radius R sin phi of the parallel. Where the meridian and parallel meet, a small shaded patch. Right: the patch enlarged as a rectangle with perpendicular tangent vectors Phi_theta along the parallel and Phi_phi along the meridian; its sides are labeled R sin phi d theta and R d phi.","caption":"A small step $d\\phi$ moves you along a meridian and a small step $d\\theta$ along a parallel. The two sides are perpendicular, with lengths $R\\,d\\phi$ and $R\\sin\\phi\\,d\\theta$.","file":"w07-p1-a"}},{"expected":"2*pi*6371*h","units":"km²","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Integrate the area element from (a) over the band, then change variable from $\\phi$ to the height $z$.","With $z=R\\cos\\phi$, the area element $R^2\\sin\\phi\\,d\\phi\\,d\\theta$ becomes $R\\,dz\\,d\\theta$. Check: $h=2R$ must give the whole sphere, $4\\pi R^2$, about 510 million km²."],"id":"w07-p1-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: side view of the sphere cut by two horizontal planes z = z1 and z = z1 + h, the band between them shaded, and one thin horizontal strip at height z, with the angle phi from the z-axis to the strip. Right: the strip edge enlarged as a right triangle: the slanted side R d phi along the meridian, the vertical side |dz| = R sin phi d phi, and the angle phi between the slanted side and the horizontal.","caption":"Slice the band into thin strips. Each strip is a circle of radius $R\\sin\\phi$ and slanted width $R\\,d\\phi$, and its height is $|dz|=R\\sin\\phi\\,d\\phi$, so the strip area can be written with $dz$ instead of $d\\phi$.","file":"w07-p1-b"}},{"expected":"sin(23.44*pi/180)","units":"","kind":"number","tolerance":0.002,"hints":["The tropics are a band like the one in (b). Find its height $h$ from the latitude $23.44^\\circ$.","The band runs from $z=-R\\sin23.44^\\circ$ to $z=R\\sin23.44^\\circ$. Divide its area by the whole sphere’s area $4\\pi R^2$; the fraction must lie between 0 and 1."],"id":"w07-p1-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: side view of the sphere with the equator, a radius to the Tropic of Cancer at latitude 23.44 degrees above the equator, the vertical drop z = R sin 23.44 degrees, and the angle phi from the North Pole. The tropics band is shaded. Right: a vertical height ruler from -R (South Pole) to R (North Pole), with the tropics marked from z1 = -R sin 23.44 degrees to z2 = R sin 23.44 degrees.","caption":"Latitude is measured from the equator, so the edge of the tropics sits at height $z=R\\sin23.44^\\circ$. The tropics are a band like the one in (b), from $z_1=-R\\sin23.44^\\circ$ to $z_2=R\\sin23.44^\\circ$.","file":"w07-p1-c"}}]},{"id":"w07-p2","questions":[{"expected":"sqrt(1+r^2/92.16)","units":"","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The factor compares a small tilted patch of mirror with its flat shadow. Differentiate $g(x,y)=(x^2+y^2)/19.2$ with respect to $x$ and to $y$.","$g_x=x/9.6$ and $g_y=y/9.6$. Then use $x^2+y^2=r^2$."],"id":"w07-p2-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: cross-section of the right half of the dish, from the vertex to the rim, with the aperture plane dashed at rim height. Two mirror pieces with equal 1 m shadows are highlighted: near the flat vertex the piece is about as long as its shadow; near the steep rim the piece is longer. Right: an enlarged right triangle with a 1 m horizontal side (the shadow), a vertical rise equal to the slope, the norm of grad g, and the slanted mirror side.","caption":"A piece of mirror is longer than its shadow on the aperture by the slanted side of a triangle with run 1 and rise $\\|\\nabla g\\|=\\sqrt{g_x^2+g_y^2}$, the slope in the steepest direction.","file":"w07-p2-a"}},{"expected":"2*pi*92.16/3*((1+36/92.16)^(3/2)-1)","units":"m²","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Use polar coordinates: multiply the factor from (a) by $r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $0\\le r\\le6$ and $0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$.","Substitute $w=1+r^2/92.16$, so $dw=2r\\,dr/92.16$. Check: the answer must exceed the flat aperture’s $36\\pi\\approx113.1$ m²."],"id":"w07-p2-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the parameter domain, a rectangle with r from 0 to 6 m across and theta from 0 to 2 pi up, with one small rectangle dr d theta shaded. Right: its image, the aperture disk of radius 6 m, with the matching small polar piece shaded and labeled area r dr d theta, and the angle theta marked.","caption":"The polar domain $0\\le r\\le6$ m, $0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$ covers the aperture disk. A small $dr\\,d\\theta$ rectangle becomes a polar piece of area $r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$; the mirror above it has the factor from (a) times that area.","file":"w07-p2-b"}},{"expected":"sqrt(19.2*x+92.16)","units":"m","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Bring $y$ inside the square root: $y\\sqrt{1+y'^2}=\\sqrt{y^2+y^2y'^2}$.","Since $y'=19.2/(2y)$, $y^2y'^2=19.2^2/4$. Then replace $y^2$ by $19.2x$."],"id":"w07-p2-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the dish on its side, drawn to scale: the profile y = sqrt(19.2 x) and its mirror image, with a thin band highlighted and a curved arrow showing rotation about the x-axis. Right: a plot of y = sqrt(19.2 x) for x from 0 to 1.875 m with a short highlighted piece of the curve at x = 1, its height y, its horizontal width dx, its slanted length ds = sqrt(1 + y prime squared) dx, and a note that the tangent is vertical at x = 0.","caption":"Rotating a short piece of the profile about the $x$-axis sweeps a thin band of radius $y$ and slanted width $ds=\\sqrt{1+y'^2}\\,dx$. The slope $y'$ blows up at $x=0$, so simplify $y\\sqrt{1+y'^2}$ before integrating.","file":"w07-p2-c"}}]},{"id":"w07-p3","questions":[{"expected":["0.5","0"],"units":"","kind":"vector","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Match the third coordinate first: $z=ct$.","With $t$ known, solve the $x$ and $y$ equations for $\\cos s$ and $\\sin s$. The answer is not the polar angle of $(38,19)$."],"id":"w07-p3-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: side view of the tower profile with a height axis marked ground t = -sqrt 3, waist t = 0, and top t = 2.5 - sqrt 3, using z = ct with c = 80 m, and the marker P at height z = 40 m. Right: top view with the dashed waist circle of radius a and a larger circle at height z = ct. From the point at angle s on the waist circle, an arrow of length a t runs along the tangent direction to the point Phi(t, s), whose polar angle is not s.","caption":"The height fixes $t$ through $z=ct$. Seen from above, $\\Phi(t,s)$ starts at the waist point at angle $s$ and moves a distance $at$ along the tangent, so the polar angle of $P$ is not $s$.","file":"w07-p3-a"}},{"expected":["-3040","-1520","722"],"units":"m²","kind":"vector","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Differentiate $\\Phi$ with respect to $t$ and to $s$, then substitute the parameters from (a).","At $P$ you should get $\\Phi_t=(0,38,80)$ and $\\Phi_s=(-19,38,0)$. Check: $\\mathbf N\\cdot\\Phi_t=\\mathbf N\\cdot\\Phi_s=0$."],"id":"w07-p3-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"The tower drawn with faint straight lines. Highlighted through the marker P: one straight line of the surface (s fixed, t varying) in teal and the horizontal circle through P (t fixed, s varying) in brown. At P, arrow Phi_t points along the straight line and arrow Phi_s points along the circle.","caption":"$\\Phi_t$ points along the straight line through $P$ ($s$ fixed), and $\\Phi_s$ points along the horizontal circle through $P$ ($t$ fixed). Their cross product $\\mathbf N$ is perpendicular to both.","file":"w07-p3-b"}},{"expected":"(80*x+40*y-3040)/19","units":"m","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["A plane through $P$ with normal $\\mathbf N$ is $\\mathbf N\\cdot(\\mathbf X-P)=0$.","Divide $\\mathbf N$ by 38 to get smaller numbers, then solve for $z$. Check: $x=38$, $y=19$ must give $z=40$."],"id":"w07-p3-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"A close-up of the shell near P, drawn as a mesh of straight lines and horizontal circles, with a translucent orange tangent plane through P. An arrow N leaves P perpendicular to the plane, and an arrow from P to a point X in the plane lies in the plane.","caption":"The tangent plane at $P$ is spanned by $\\Phi_t$ and $\\Phi_s$. For every point $\\mathbf X=(x,y,z)$ in it, $\\mathbf X-P$ lies in the plane, so $\\mathbf N\\cdot(\\mathbf X-P)=0$.","file":"w07-p3-c"}}]},{"id":"w07-p4","questions":[{"expected":["6*u*cos(v)","6*u*sin(v)","u"],"units":"m","kind":"vector","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Differentiate $\\Phi$ with respect to $u$ and to $v$.","$\\Phi_u=(\\cos v,\\sin v,-6)$ and $\\Phi_v=(-u\\sin v,u\\cos v,0)$. Check: $\\mathbf N$ should point up and away from the axis."],"id":"w07-p4-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: side view in the plane v = 0, to scale: the tower face as a slanted line from radius 2.75 m at the ground to 1.25 m at 9 m, dashed up to the apex u = 0. At a point at distance u from the axis, arrow Phi_u points along the slant, 1 m out and 6 m down. Right: top view of the shadow annulus with the circle u fixed dashed, the angle v, and at a point two arrows: Phi_v tangent to the circle with length u, and Phi_u pointing straight away from the axis.","caption":"$\\Phi_u$ runs down the slope (1 m out for every 6 m down), and $\\Phi_v$ runs around the circle of radius $u$, with length $u$.","file":"w07-p4-a"}},{"expected":"0","units":"","kind":"choice","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Regularity fails exactly where the cross product is the zero vector.","Its norm is $u\\sqrt{37}$. Find where that vanishes, then compare with the tower’s range of $u$."],"options":["Only at u = 0, the apex 7.5 m above the tower; the tower itself is regular everywhere.","Along the top rim u = 1.25, where the tower is cut off.","Along the seam v = 0, where the parametrization starts and ends.","Nowhere: a cone has no special point."],"id":"w07-p4-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the parameter rectangle u from 0 to 2.75 m, v from 0 to 2 pi, with the tower strip 1.25 to 2.75 shaded. Equal small squares are drawn at u = 0.3, 1.3, and 2.25, and one square is split between the bottom edge v = 0 and the top edge v = 2 pi. Right: the top view of the cone, where the same squares become pieces of different sizes: tiny near the apex u = 0, larger farther out, and one piece straddling the seam v = 0.","caption":"Equal squares $du\\,dv$ in the domain become pieces of different sizes on the cone. Compare a square near $u=0$, squares in the tower, and the one that is split across the seam $v=0$, $v=2\\pi$.","file":"w07-p4-b"}},{"expected":"6*pi*sqrt(37)","units":"m²","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Integrate $\\|\\Phi_u\\times\\Phi_v\\|=u\\sqrt{37}$ over the tower’s part of the domain.","Use $1.25\\le u\\le2.75$ and $0\\le v\\le2\\pi$. Size check: the face is about 9 m up the slope and 8 to 17 m around, so expect roughly 100 m²."],"id":"w07-p4-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the parameter rectangle with the tower part, 1.25 <= u <= 2.75 and 0 <= v <= 2 pi, shaded, and the tip part 0 <= u < 1.25 unshaded. An arrow labeled Phi points to the right, where the truncated cone is drawn in 3D with its grid curves, the top rim at u = 1.25, the base at u = 2.75, and the imaginary tip dotted.","caption":"Only the shaded part of the domain, $1.25\\le u\\le2.75$ and $0\\le v\\le2\\pi$, becomes stone. Integrate $\\|\\Phi_u\\times\\Phi_v\\|\\,du\\,dv$ over that rectangle.","file":"w07-p4-c"}}]},{"id":"w07-p5","questions":[{"expected":"2*(6.2+2*cos(phi))","units":"m²","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Compute $\\Phi_\\theta$ and $\\Phi_\\phi$ and check that their dot product is zero.","Their lengths are $R+r\\cos\\phi$ and $r$."],"id":"w07-p5-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: a cut through the central axis showing one circular tube cross-section of radius r around the tube center, a point at angle phi, a short arc r d phi there, and the horizontal distance R + r cos phi from the axis to the point. Right: top view: the point travels around the axis on a circle of radius R + r cos phi; a short arc of angle d theta on it is labeled (R + r cos phi) d theta.","caption":"A point at angle $\\phi$ on the tube is $R+r\\cos\\phi$ from the central axis. A small patch has sides $r\\,d\\phi$ around the tube and $(R+r\\cos\\phi)\\,d\\theta$ around the axis, and they are perpendicular.","file":"w07-p5-a"}},{"expected":"4*pi^2*6.2*2","units":"m²","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Integrate $r(R+r\\cos\\phi)$ over both angles, each from $0$ to $2\\pi$.","The $\\cos\\phi$ term integrates to zero. Size check: expect a few hundred m²."],"id":"w07-p5-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the square parameter domain, theta and phi each from 0 to 2 pi, with one small cell d theta d phi. Right: the graph of cos phi for phi from 0 to 2 pi, with the area above the axis shaded teal and marked plus, and the area below shaded orange and marked minus.","caption":"Integrate over the whole square $0\\le\\theta,\\phi\\le2\\pi$. The $\\cos\\phi$ term is positive and negative over equal areas, so it contributes nothing.","file":"w07-p5-b"}},{"expected":"32*pi","units":"m²","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The outer half has $-\\pi/2\\le\\phi\\le\\pi/2$ and the inner half $\\pi/2\\le\\phi\\le3\\pi/2$.","The $R$ terms are equal for the two halves. Over the outer half $\\int\\cos\\phi\\,d\\phi=2$; over the inner half it is $-2$."],"id":"w07-p5-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: a cut through the axis showing one tube cross-section, with its outer half (farther from the axis, from phi = -pi/2 to pi/2) in teal and its inner half in orange. Right: top view of the torus with the outer half as an outer teal ring (cos phi > 0) and the inner half as an inner orange ring (cos phi < 0).","caption":"The outer half is $-\\pi/2\\le\\phi\\le\\pi/2$, where $\\cos\\phi>0$, and the inner half is $\\pi/2\\le\\phi\\le3\\pi/2$. Both halves run all the way around the axis, $0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$.","file":"w07-p5-c"}}]}]},{"week":8,"problems":[{"id":"w08-p1","questions":[{"expected":"4*pi*149.6^2*1360.8/10^6","units":"YW","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["On this sphere $\\mathbf F$ points along $\\mathbf n=\\mathbf r/a$, so $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n$ is the constant $Sd^2/a^2$.","Multiply that constant by the sphere’s area $4\\pi a^2$, with $d=1.496\\times10^{11}$ m. Expect a few hundred YW, close to the published luminosity."],"id":"w08-p1-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the Sun at the center of a circle of radius a, with straight rays pointing outward through it; a small patch dS on the circle carries an outward normal arrow n. Right: the patch enlarged, with n = r/a and the sunlight F drawn as parallel arrows pointing the same way, labeled: the length of F is S d squared over a squared everywhere on the sphere","caption":"On the sphere $|\\mathbf r|=a$ the sunlight $\\mathbf F$ points along $\\mathbf n=\\mathbf r/a$ and has the same size on every patch $dS$.","file":"w08-p1-a"}},{"expected":"pi*a^3","units":"","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Use $z=a\\cos\\phi$ and $dS=a^2\\sin\\phi\\,d\\phi\\,d\\theta$; the upper hemisphere is $0\\le\\phi\\le\\pi/2$.","The integral of $\\cos\\phi\\sin\\phi$ from $0$ to $\\pi/2$ is $1/2$. A height times an area gives length cubed."],"id":"w08-p1-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: side view of the upper hemisphere of radius a, with the angle phi measured down from the vertical axis, phi = 0 at the top and phi = pi/2 on the rim, and the height of a point marked as z = a cos phi. Right: the parameter rectangle, phi from 0 to pi/2 across and theta from 0 to 2 pi up, with one small cell labeled dS = a squared sin phi dphi dtheta","caption":"The upper hemisphere is $0\\le\\phi\\le\\pi/2$, $0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$; a point at angle $\\phi$ has height $z=a\\cos\\phi$, and each small cell has area $dS=a^2\\sin\\phi\\,d\\phi\\,d\\theta$.","file":"w08-p1-b"}},{"expected":"1360.8/4","units":"W/m²","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["For one small patch of the day side, $(Sz/R)\\,dS$ is the power it absorbs; adding the patches gives $S/R$ times your part (b) answer with $a=R$.","Divide that power by the area of the whole sphere, $\\iint 1\\,dS=4\\pi R^2$. Expect well under $S$: the night side gets nothing, and most of the day side is lit at a slant."],"id":"w08-p1-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Earth as a circle in vertical sunlight. The day half is shaded from deep gold at the top, where the Sun is overhead and Sz/R = S, to pale at the day-night line, where Sz/R = 0; the night half is grey and labeled 0. A note says to average over day and night by dividing by the whole area 4 pi R squared","caption":"The absorbed power per square meter, $Sz/R$, is $S$ where the Sun is overhead, falls to $0$ at the day–night line, and is $0$ on the night side. Average over the whole sphere, area $4\\pi R^2$.","file":"w08-p1-c"}}]},{"id":"w08-p2","questions":[{"expected":["-x/3.4","0","1"],"units":"","kind":"vector","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Differentiate $g$ with respect to $x$ and to $y$; the trough is straight along $y$.","$g_y=0$, so only $g_x$ needs simplifying; keep $+1$ as the third component for the upward orientation."],"id":"w08-p2-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the right half of the trough cross-section z = x squared over 6.8, with a dashed tangent line at x = 1.7, a slope triangle with run 1 and rise g_x, and an orange arrow (minus g_x, 1) perpendicular to the curve. Right: a view along the trough, where the mirror height at the bottom, at x = 1.7, and at the rim are horizontal lines: the height does not change with y","caption":"Across the trough the slope is $g_x$ and $(-g_x,1)$ is perpendicular to the mirror; along the trough the height never changes, which tells you $g_y$.","file":"w08-p2-a"}},{"expected":"900*1308000/10^6","units":"MW","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["For one small strip of mirror, $f\\,dS=900\\cos\\alpha\\,dS$, and $\\cos\\alpha\\,dS$ is the area of the strip’s shadow: the cosine cancels the area factor.","So the power is $900$ W/m$^2$ times the shadow area, which is the aperture area, summed over the whole field. Expect an answer of order a thousand MW."],"id":"w08-p2-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"The right half of the trough cross-section with vertical sunbeams falling on one short strip of mirror, highlighted in orange. Directly above the strip, its shadow on the aperture line is a short dark segment labeled dA = cos alpha dS. Labels give the sunlight as 900 W per square meter straight down and the power on the strip as 900 cos alpha dS","caption":"Each strip of mirror gets $900\\cos\\alpha\\,dS$ watts, and its shadow on the aperture has area $dA=\\cos\\alpha\\,dS$.","file":"w08-p2-b"}},{"expected":"2","units":"","kind":"choice","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The vector $(-g_x,-g_y,1)\\,dA$ already equals $\\mathbf n\\,dS$: it includes the area factor.","The option with the length of $\\mathbf F$ ignores the angle, so it counts light at full strength even where it strikes the mirror at a slant."],"options":["$-\\iint_D\\mathbf F\\cdot(-g_x,-g_y,1)\\sqrt{1+g_x^2+g_y^2}\\,dA$","$\\iint_D\\|\\mathbf F\\|\\sqrt{1+g_x^2+g_y^2}\\,dA$","$-\\iint_D\\mathbf F\\cdot(-g_x,-g_y,1)\\,dA$","$-\\iint_D\\mathbf F\\cdot\\dfrac{(-g_x,-g_y,1)}{\\sqrt{1+g_x^2+g_y^2}}\\,dA$"],"id":"w08-p2-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"A small tilted patch dS of the mirror above its flat shadow dA. From the patch rise two arrows in the same direction: the long vector N = (minus g_x, minus g_y, 1), whose vertical part is 1, and the unit normal n of length 1. Text: the length of N is the square root of 1 + g_x squared + g_y squared, the patch area is dS = that square root times dA, so N dA = n dS","caption":"$\\mathbf N=(-g_x,-g_y,1)$ has length $\\sqrt{1+g_x^2+g_y^2}$, the same factor that turns $dA$ into $dS$, so $\\mathbf N\\,dA=\\mathbf n\\,dS$.","file":"w08-p2-c"}}]},{"id":"w08-p3","questions":[{"expected":["0.15*cos(theta)","0.15*sin(theta)","0"],"units":"m","kind":"vector","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Differentiate $\\Phi$ with respect to $\\theta$ and to $z$, then take the cross product in the order $\\Phi_\\theta\\times\\Phi_z$.","$\\Phi_\\theta=(-0.15\\sin\\theta,0.15\\cos\\theta,0)$ and $\\Phi_z=(0,0,1)$."],"id":"w08-p3-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: side view of the body cylinder with a point at height z on its side and an orange arrow Phi_z pointing straight up. Right: the cylinder from above, with x and y axes, a point at angle theta on the circle, and an orange arrow Phi_theta tangent to the circle in the counterclockwise direction; at the point, Phi_z points up, out of the page","caption":"$\\Phi_z$ points straight up and $\\Phi_\\theta$ is tangent to the circle, counterclockwise seen from above. Their cross product is perpendicular to both.","file":"w08-p3-a"}},{"expected":"-10^6*2*0.15*1.7*(0.0152/3600/6.7)*1.5","units":"mL/s","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["On the side, $\\mathbf F\\cdot(\\Phi_\\theta\\times\\Phi_z)=-0.15\\rho v\\cos\\theta$; integrate over $-\\pi/2\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/2$ and $0\\le z\\le1.7$.","Use $\\rho=R/u$ with $R=0.0152/3600$ m/s and $u=6.7$ m/s, then convert m$^3$ to mL. Expect a negative answer smaller than $1$ mL/s in size."],"id":"w08-p3-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the cylinder from above, with the front half highlighted from theta = minus pi/2 to pi/2, horizontal rain arrows (minus v) arriving from the right, and outward normals on the front; the label says F dot n is negative there. Right: the front half unrolled into a rectangle, theta from minus pi/2 to pi/2 across and z from 0 to 1.7 m up, shaded darkest in the middle where cos theta is largest, with a small cell dS = 0.15 dtheta dz","caption":"On the front half the rain comes in, so $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n<0$, most strongly at $\\theta=0$. Integrate over $-\\pi/2\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/2$, $0\\le z\\le1.7$.","file":"w08-p3-b"}},{"expected":"10^8*(0.0152/3600)*(pi*0.15^2/v+2*0.15*1.7/6.7)","units":"mL","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The trip takes $100/v$ seconds; multiply the water landing on you per second by this time.","Per second, $R$ times the disk area lands on the top, and $\\rho v\\cdot2(0.15)(1.7)$ lands on the front. At walking speed expect a few tens of mL."],"id":"w08-p3-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Side view of the whole 100 m trip, not to scale: a dashed outline of your body at the start and a solid one at the end. A long blue slab between them is the rainy air your front sweeps through, 100 m long, 2(0.15) m wide and 1.7 m tall, with water fraction rho. Rain falls on the top at rate R on area pi (0.15) squared for 100/v seconds","caption":"Two contributions: the top catches rain at rate $R$ for $100/v$ seconds, and the front sweeps through a slab of rainy air $100$ m long.","file":"w08-p3-c"}}]},{"id":"w08-p4","questions":[{"expected":"2*pi*0.0689*a^3","units":"Mpc³/Gyr","kind":"expression","tolerance":0.002,"hints":["On the dome $\\mathbf n=\\mathbf r/a$, so $\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf n=Ha$ everywhere: on each small patch, $Ha\\,dS$ is the volume per Gyr leaving through it.","Multiply by the hemisphere’s area $2\\pi a^2$. Units check: Mpc/Gyr times Mpc$^2$ gives Mpc$^3$/Gyr."],"id":"w08-p4-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Cross-section of the half-ball W with the Milky Way at the center of the base. Grey velocity arrows v = H r point straight out from the dome, all the same length; a small patch dS on the dome carries the outward normal n = r/a. Text: on the dome the length of r is a, and the dome area is 2 pi a squared","caption":"On the dome $|\\mathbf r|=a$, so every arrow $\\mathbf v=H\\mathbf r$ has the same length and points along $\\mathbf n=\\mathbf r/a$.","file":"w08-p4-a"}},{"expected":"3*0.0689","units":"per Gyr","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["On the base the outward normal is $(0,0,-1)$, and $\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf n=-Hz=0$ there.","The half-ball’s volume is $\\tfrac23\\pi a^3$. The ratio has units of Gyr$^{-1}$, so $a$ must cancel."],"decimals":3,"id":"w08-p4-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Cross-section of the half-ball W. Plus signs line the dome, where v dot n is positive. On the base, orange outward normals point straight down, n = (0, 0, minus 1), and grey velocity arrows lie flat along the base, so v dot n = minus H z = 0. The volume of W is two thirds pi a cubed","caption":"Outward $\\mathbf n$ on the base points down, and $\\mathbf v$ lies along the base, so only the dome contributes. The volume of $W$ is $\\frac23\\pi a^3$.","file":"w08-p4-b"}},{"expected":"4*pi*0.0689*a^3","units":"Mpc³/Gyr","kind":"expression","tolerance":0.002,"hints":["On this sphere $\\mathbf n=(\\mathbf r-\\mathbf p)/a$, so $\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf n=Ha+H\\,\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf n$.","The constant field $H\\mathbf p$ has zero net flux through the sphere, because $\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf n$ changes sign between opposite points."],"id":"w08-p4-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Two copies of a circle centered on another galaxy at p. Left: the part H(r minus p) of the velocity, arrows pointing radially out from p, just as they do about the Milky Way. Right: the constant part H p, parallel arrows all pointing the same way; the half of the circle they leave through is marked plus and the opposite half minus, with outward normals n and minus n at two opposite points","caption":"Split $\\mathbf v=H(\\mathbf r-\\mathbf p)+H\\mathbf p$: the first part is radial from $\\mathbf p$; the constant part $H\\mathbf p$ leaves through one half of the sphere and enters through the other.","file":"w08-p4-c"}}]},{"id":"w08-p5","questions":[{"expected":"182*10^9/(3*10^6*5*10^3)","units":"kg/(m²·yr)","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The average flux is the yearly mass divided by the curtain’s area; $182$ million tonnes is $1.82\\times10^{11}$ kg.","The curtain is $3000$ km by $5$ km; convert both to meters before dividing. Expect roughly ten kilograms per square meter per year."],"id":"w08-p5-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"The 15 degrees west curtain seen from the east, drawn as a long rectangle 3000 km wide (south to north) and 5 km tall, not to scale. Symbols show dust moving away from the viewer, westward. One small orange square marks a single square meter. Text: 182 million tonnes per year cross the whole curtain; 1 km = 1000 m and 1 tonne = 1000 kg","caption":"The average flux is the yearly mass through the whole curtain divided by its area; convert km to m and tonnes to kg first.","file":"w08-p5-a"}},{"expected":"182","units":"Mt/yr","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["With the westward element $(-1,g_y,g_z)\\,dy\\,dz$, $\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf S=f(y,z)\\,dy\\,dz$.","Compare this integrand and its $(y,z)$ rectangle with the vertical curtain’s."],"id":"w08-p5-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"View from the south, x east and z up in km. The vertical curtain x = 0 is dashed; the tilted curtain x = minus z/5 leans 1 km west at its top, 5 km up. Horizontal dust arrows F = (minus f, 0, 0) cross both. An orange westward normal points out of the tilted curtain toward the west. Text: Phi(y, z) = (minus z/5, y, z) over the same (y, z) rectangle","caption":"The tilted curtain $x=-z/5$ uses the same $(y,z)$ rectangle as the vertical one; take its normal pointing west and compare the integrands.","file":"w08-p5-b"}},{"expected":"182-132","units":"Mt/yr","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Outward flux: $-182$ through the east face (dust enters) and $+132$ through the west face.","The bottom’s outward normal points down; the top and sides carry nothing in the model."],"id":"w08-p5-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Cross-section of the closed box between the curtains, with orange outward normals on all four drawn faces. Dust arrows enter through the east face (15 degrees west, outward flux minus 182) and leave through the west face (35 degrees west, outward flux plus 132). The top carries 0, and the bottom carries the unknown D, outward meaning down into the ocean. Text: steady state, total outward flux = 0","caption":"Add the outward fluxes face by face: $-182$ (east), $+132$ (west), $0$ (top and sides), and $D$ (bottom). In steady state they sum to zero.","file":"w08-p5-c"}}]}]},{"week":9,"problems":[{"id":"w09-p1","questions":[{"expected":"24*pi","units":"cm²","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Substitute the parametrization: $dx=-6\\sin t\\,dt$ and $dy=4\\cos t\\,dt$.","The integrand simplifies to $x\\,dy-y\\,dx=24\\,dt$. Integrate over $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$, then halve. Size check: the ellipse fits in a 12 cm by 8 cm box, so expect about three quarters of 96 cm$^2$."],"id":"w09-p1-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"The calibration ellipse x = 6 cos t, y = 4 sin t, with semi-axes 6 cm and 4 cm, arrows showing counterclockwise travel, and the points t = 0, pi/2, pi, and 3pi/2 marked. A thin shaded wedge from the origin to a short piece of the ellipse is labeled with area one half of (x dy minus y dx). Beside it are dx = −6 sin t dt and dy = 4 cos t dt.","caption":"Each short step along the ellipse sweeps a thin wedge from the origin of area $\\tfrac12(x\\,dy-y\\,dx)$. Going once counterclockwise, the wedges fill the ellipse exactly once.","file":"w09-p1-a"}},{"expected":"11.3","units":"cm","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Apply Green’s theorem with $P=-y/L$ and $Q=\\sqrt{L^2-y^2}/L$. Since $Q$ depends on $y$ only, $Q_x-P_y=1/L$.","So the roll is $A/L$, with $A$ the area of $T$: half the cross product of the edge vectors from M to S and from M to B."],"id":"w09-p1-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Triangle T with vertices M at the origin, S at (14, −8), and B at (16, 7), its edges arrowed counterclockwise M to S to B. The edge vectors from M to S, (14, −8), and from M to B, (16, 7), are highlighted, and a dashed parallelogram they span has twice the area of T. Dotted lines at y = 10 and y = −10 mark how far the tracer can go from the rail.","caption":"Since $Q_x-P_y=1/L$, the roll is $A/L$. The area $A$ is half the parallelogram spanned by the edge vectors from M, and the whole triangle lies inside $|y|<L$.","file":"w09-p1-b"}},{"expected":"2260","units":"cm³","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Split $\\partial T$ into the edges M to S, S to B, and B to M, and parametrize each for $0\\le t\\le1$.","For example, on S to B, $x=14+2t$ and $dy=15\\,dt$, so that piece is $15\\int_0^1(14+2t)^2\\,dt=3380$. The other two edges are negative."],"id":"w09-p1-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Triangle T with each edge labeled by its parametrization for 0 ≤ t ≤ 1: M to S is (14t, −8t) with dy = −8 dt, S to B is (14 + 2t, −8 + 15t) with dy = 15 dt, and B to M is (16 − 16t, 7 − 7t) with dy = −7 dt. The two edges running south are drawn in one color, and the single edge running north in another.","caption":"Only the east edge S to B runs north ($dy>0$), and it is also where $x^2$ is largest. The other two edges run south and contribute negative amounts.","file":"w09-p1-c"}}]},{"id":"w09-p2","questions":[{"expected":"1239.5*pi","units":"m²","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Green’s theorem turns $\\tfrac12\\oint_{\\partial D}x\\,dy-y\\,dx$ into $\\iint_D1\\,dA$.","In polar coordinates this is $\\int_0^\\pi\\int_{15}^{52}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$. Size check: the whole half-disk of radius 52 m is about 4,250 m$^2$, and the orchestra takes away a little."],"id":"w09-p2-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"The half-ring seating region between radii 15 m and 52 m above the stage line, with a radius ray, an arc showing theta running from 0 to pi, and one small polar box shaded and labeled dA = r dr dtheta.","caption":"In polar coordinates $D$ is $15\\le r\\le52$, $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi$, and a small piece has area $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$.","file":"w09-p2-a"}},{"expected":"-2250","units":"m³","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Keeping $D$ on the left, the inner arc runs clockwise, from $(-15,0)$ to $(15,0)$.","Use $x=15\\cos t$, $y=15\\sin t$ with $t$ going from $\\pi$ down to $0$. Then $-\\tfrac12y^2\\,dx=\\tfrac{15^3}{2}\\sin^3t\\,dt$, and $\\int_0^\\pi\\sin^3t\\,dt=\\tfrac43$."],"id":"w09-p2-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the boundary of the half-ring with arrows: along the stage line from 15 to 52, counterclockwise around the outer arc, along the stage line from −52 to −15, and clockwise around the inner arc, so the region is always on the left. Right: a close-up of the inner arc from (−15, 0) at t = pi, through t = pi/2 at the top, to (15, 0) at t = 0, with dx > 0.","caption":"Keeping $D$ on the left makes the inner arc run clockwise, from $(-15,0)$ to $(15,0)$: use $x=15\\cos t$, $y=15\\sin t$ with $t$ running from $\\pi$ down to $0$.","file":"w09-p2-b"}},{"expected":"2/3*(52^3-15^3)","units":"m³","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Green’s theorem with $P=-y^2/2$ and $Q=0$ gives $Q_x-P_y=y$.","In polar coordinates $y\\,dA=(r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, so $\\iint_Dy\\,dA=\\int_0^\\pi\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta\\int_{15}^{52}r^2\\,dr$."],"id":"w09-p2-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"The half-ring shaded from light near the stage line to dark at the back, showing that the weight y grows with distance from the stage line. One small piece dA is marked with a dashed vertical segment showing its distance y = r sin theta from the line.","caption":"The first moment weights each small piece $dA$ by its distance $y=r\\sin\\theta$ from the stage line, so $M=\\iint_D y\\,dA$ separates into a $\\theta$ integral and an $r$ integral.","file":"w09-p2-c"}}]},{"id":"w09-p3","questions":[{"expected":"27000*pi","units":"km²/h","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["On this circle the wind blows along the loop, counterclockwise, at the calibrated 90 km/h, so each kilometer of the loop contributes 90 km$^2$/h.","For a tangent wind of constant speed, circulation $=$ speed $\\times$ length of the loop. Size check: expect km$^2$/h and a value near 100,000."],"id":"w09-p3-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"The circle of radius 150 km around the eye, with wind arrows of equal length all pointing counterclockwise along the circle. One short piece ds of the circle is highlighted with the note that the wind along it, at 90 km/h, adds 90 ds.","caption":"On this circle the wind is tangent and has the same speed, $90$ km/h, everywhere, so $\\mathbf v\\cdot d\\mathbf r=90\\,ds$ on every piece of the loop.","file":"w09-p3-a"}},{"expected":"-27000*pi","units":"km²/h","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Between the ellipse and a small circle around the eye the curl is zero, so by Green’s theorem the two loops, both taken counterclockwise, have equal circulation.","Traversing the ellipse clockwise reverses the sign."],"id":"w09-p3-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"The ellipse with semi-axes 300 km and 200 km, arrowed counterclockwise, and a small circle C epsilon around the eye arrowed clockwise. The region R epsilon between them is shaded and labeled with zero curl.","caption":"The region $R_\\varepsilon$ between the ellipse and a small circle $C_\\varepsilon$ avoids the eye and has $Q_x-P_y=0$. Its positive boundary is the ellipse counterclockwise plus $C_\\varepsilon$ clockwise.","file":"w09-p3-b"}},{"expected":"0","units":"km²/h","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Check whether this circle encloses the eye.","The field is smooth on the disk this circle bounds, and its curl is zero there."],"id":"w09-p3-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Wind arrows circling the eye, which is marked with an x where the wind is undefined. The circle of radius 100 km centered 420 km east is shaded; it lies 320 km from the eye and does not contain it.","caption":"The disk bounded by the loop in (c) stays $320$ km away from the eye, so $\\mathbf v$ is smooth on the whole disk and Green’s theorem applies.","file":"w09-p3-c"}}]},{"id":"w09-p4","questions":[{"expected":"4*pi*r^4/3","units":"","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["By symmetry the integrals of $x^2$, $y^2$, and $z^2$ over the sphere are equal, and together they add up to the integral of $x^2+y^2+z^2=r^2$.","The sphere’s area is $4\\pi r^2$, so the three integrals add up to $r^2\\cdot4\\pi r^2$."],"id":"w09-p4-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: a sphere of radius r with x, y, and z axes and a curved arrow showing the relabeling x to y to z. Right: three equal blocks labeled with the integrals of x squared, y squared, and z squared over S, stacked above one block for the integral of x squared plus y squared plus z squared, which equals the integral of r squared.","caption":"Relabeling the axes maps the sphere to itself, so the three integrals are equal, and together they make $\\iint_S r^2\\,dS$ over a sphere of area $4\\pi r^2$.","file":"w09-p4-a"}},{"expected":"4*pi*2.16^3/3","units":"L","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["On the sphere, $\\mathbf n=(x,y,z)/R$, so the integrand is $z^2/R$.","Use part (a) with $R=432/2$ mm $=2.16$ dm. Size check: a ball fills about half of the cube around it, and that cube holds $4.32^3\\approx81$ liters."],"id":"w09-p4-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Cross-section of the float with the z-axis vertical. At points around the sphere, outward normal arrows n point away from the center, and field arrows (0, 0, z) point up on the top half and down on the bottom half, with zero length at the equator. Plus signs mark both halves.","caption":"On top, $(0,0,z)$ and $\\mathbf n$ both point up; underneath, both point down. So $(0,0,z)\\cdot\\mathbf n=z^2/R\\ge0$ everywhere on $S$, with $\\mathbf n=(x,y,z)/R$.","file":"w09-p4-b"}},{"expected":"16*(1.025*4*pi*2.16^3/3-17)","units":"kg","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Seawater has density 1.025 kg per liter, so one float displaces 1.025 times the liters from part (b), in kilograms.","Subtract each float’s 17 kg, then multiply by 16."],"id":"w09-p4-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"One float with an upward arrow labeled up-thrust, rho times the flux from part (b), with rho = 1.025 kg per liter, and a downward arrow labeled weight 17 kg. Beside it, a cluster of 16 floats on a mooring rope, labeled net lift times 16.","caption":"Per float, the net lift in kilograms is $\\rho$ times the volume from (b) minus the $17$ kg of glass; the cluster on M4 has $16$ floats.","file":"w09-p4-c"}}]},{"id":"w09-p5","questions":[{"expected":"2*6.6/90*(1-r^2/8100)","units":"1/h","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Write $\\mathbf u=g(r)\\,(-y,x)$ with $g(r)=\\tfrac{6.6}{90}\\big(1-\\tfrac{r^2}{16200}\\big)$, and use the product rule.","For a field $g(r)\\,(-y,x)$, $Q_x-P_y=2g+r\\,g^{\\prime}(r)$."],"id":"w09-p5-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the ring seen from above, with counterclockwise arrows that grow and then shrink with distance from the center. Right: a graph of g(r), starting at 6.6/90 per hour at the center and falling to zero at r = 90 root 2 km, with a tangent line marking the slope g prime of r.","caption":"Write $\\mathbf u=g(r)\\,(-y,x)$, with $g$ from the model. The product rule gives a term $2g$ and a term with the slope $g^{\\prime}(r)$ shown here.","file":"w09-p5-a"}},{"expected":"594*pi","units":"km²/h","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["By Green’s theorem the circulation is the integral of $Q_x-P_y$ over the enclosed region, and $Q_x-P_y$ is positive exactly for $r<90$ km.","Integrate $Q_x-P_y$ over that disk, or use circulation $=2\\pi r\\,v(r)$ at $r=90$ km. Size check: a few km/h around a loop about 570 km long gives a couple of thousand km$^2$/h."],"id":"w09-p5-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"The ring from above, with the core disk r < 90 km shaded and marked plus, and the outer band from 90 km to 90 root 2 km shaded and marked minus. Two counterclockwise candidate loops are drawn: a small dashed circle inside the core and a larger dash-dot circle that reaches into the outer band.","caption":"Circulation $=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,dA$. A loop inside the core misses some of the positive part; a loop reaching into the outer band picks up negative part.","file":"w09-p5-b"}},{"expected":"0","units":"km²/h","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["At the edge the swirl speed is zero.","Equivalently, the negative $Q_x-P_y$ in the outer band cancels the positive core."],"id":"w09-p5-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"The ring from above with the positive core and negative outer band shaded, the edge circle r = 90 root 2 km drawn thick with a counterclockwise arrow, and swirl arrows that shrink toward the edge.","caption":"The edge loop $r=90\\sqrt2$ km encloses the whole core and the whole outer band. Look at the swirl speed there, or compare the two parts.","file":"w09-p5-c"}}]}]},{"week":10,"problems":[{"id":"w10-p1","questions":[{"expected":["0","0","2*w"],"units":"rad/s","kind":"vector","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["$\\mathbf v$ has no $z$-component and does not depend on $z$, so the first two components of the curl vanish.","Third component: $\\partial_x(wx)-\\partial_y(-wy)$."],"id":"w10-p1-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: arrows of the field w(-y, x, 0) in the xy-plane, circling counterclockwise around the z-axis, with a small square marked. Right: that small square enlarged, traversed counterclockwise, with the tangential components v1 at its bottom and top edges and v2 at its left and right edges labelled","caption":"The field is the same in every horizontal plane. Around a small square, the circulation per unit area tends to $\\partial_xv_2-\\partial_yv_1$, the third component of $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf v$.","file":"w10-p1-a"}},{"expected":"2*pi*7.292115e-5*6371^2*cos(36.605*pi/180)^2","units":"km²/s","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Along $C$ the velocity is tangent to $C$, with constant speed $\\Omega R\\cos L$.","Multiply that speed by the length $2\\pi R\\cos L$ of $C$, or use Stokes on the flat disk. Size check: a few tenths of a km/s times tens of thousands of km, so of order $10^4$ km$^2$/s."],"id":"w10-p1-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: a cut through Earth’s axis showing Monterey at latitude L and the horizontal segment of length R cos L from the axis to Monterey. Right: the circle C seen from above the North Pole, with velocity arrows tangent to it pointing counterclockwise (eastward)","caption":"Monterey’s parallel $C$ is a circle of radius $R\\cos L$. Seen from above the North Pole, $\\mathbf v$ is tangent to $C$, points east (counterclockwise), and has the same speed $\\Omega R\\cos L$ all the way around.","file":"w10-p1-b"}},{"expected":"1","units":"","kind":"choice","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["By part (a), $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf v=2\\Omega\\mathbf k$ at every point of Earth.","For a unit axle $\\mathbf u$ the spin is $\\Omega\\,\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf u=\\Omega\\cos\\alpha$, where $\\alpha$ is the angle between $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf k$. Find $\\alpha$ for each option."],"options":["Straight up (the local vertical at Monterey)","Parallel to Earth’s axis: pointing north, tilted up 36.6° from the horizontal","Horizontal, pointing east","All directions spin equally, because the rotation is rigid"],"id":"w10-p1-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: a paddle wheel on an axle u at angle alpha from the curl vector found in part (a), with the projection of the curl onto the axle dotted. Right: the directions at Monterey in a cut through Earth’s axis: the horizon, north, the local vertical, east out of the page, and a dashed line parallel to Earth’s axis tilted up by the angle L from north","caption":"A paddle wheel feels only the part of $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf v$ along its axle. At Monterey, Earth’s axis is tilted up by $L$ from north; find the angle $\\alpha$ to it for each candidate axle.","file":"w10-p1-c"}}]},{"id":"w10-p2","questions":[{"expected":["0","0","-b"],"units":"V/m²","kind":"vector","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["$\\mathbf E$ has no $z$-component and does not depend on $z$, so only the third component of the curl can be nonzero.","Compute $\\partial_x(-bx/2)-\\partial_y(by/2)$; Faraday’s law tells you what to expect."],"id":"w10-p2-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: arrows of the field E = (b/2)(y, -x, 0) for b > 0 in the xy-plane, circling clockwise, with a small square marked. Right: that small square enlarged, traversed counterclockwise, with the tangential components E1 and E2 on its edges labelled","caption":"$\\mathbf E$ is the same in every horizontal plane. Around a small square, the circulation per unit area tends to $\\partial_xE_2-\\partial_yE_1$, the third component of $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf E$.","file":"w10-p2-a"}},{"expected":"-0.0004*pi*b","units":"V","kind":"expression","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Parametrize the loop as $\\mathbf r(t)=(0.02\\cos t,\\,0.02\\sin t,\\,0)$; the dot product $\\mathbf E\\cdot\\mathbf r^\\prime$ comes out constant.","$\\mathbf E\\cdot\\mathbf r^\\prime=-\\tfrac b2(0.02)^2$; integrate over $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. The minus sign is Lenz’s law."],"id":"w10-p2-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"The flat loop of radius 0.02 m seen from above, with arrowheads showing counterclockwise orientation, the angle t from the x-axis to the point r(t), the tangent vector r prime of t, and blue arrows of E inside the loop circling the other way for b > 0","caption":"Parametrize the flat loop by the angle $t$. Compare the direction of $\\mathbf E$ with the direction of travel $\\mathbf r^\\prime(t)$: the dot product $\\mathbf E\\cdot\\mathbf r^\\prime$ is the same at every point.","file":"w10-p2-b"}},{"expected":"2*pi*140000*0.001*pi*0.02^2","units":"V","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Use Stokes on the planar patch $z=0.004+0.36y$ over the disk $x^2+y^2\\le0.02^2$: its upward vector area element is $(0,-0.36,1)\\,dA$.","Dotting the curl with that element gives $|\\text{EMF}|=|b|$ times the disk’s area, and $|b|=|B^\\prime(t)|$ peaks at $0.001\\cdot2\\pi\\cdot140000$ T/s. Size check: of order $1$ V."],"id":"w10-p2-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: a side view, looking along the x-axis, with y and z in millimeters: the disk of radius 0.02 m edge-on as a horizontal segment at z = 0, the planar patch z = 0.004 + 0.36 y bounded by the tilted loop C as a tilted segment, the vector area element (0, -0.36, 1) dA drawn perpendicular to the patch with its vertical and horizontal parts dotted, and dashed vertical lines for the direction of the curl of E. Right: one cycle of B(t) in millitesla over 7.14 microseconds, with orange tangent lines where the graph is steepest","caption":"The planar patch over the disk has vector area element $(0,-0.36,1)\\,dA$; only its vertical part meets the vertical curl. The EMF is largest when $B(t)$ changes fastest, where its graph is steepest.","file":"w10-p2-c"}}]},{"id":"w10-p3","questions":[{"expected":"2","units":"","kind":"choice","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The divergence must be $0$: that already rules out two options.","Of the remaining two, one has curl $(0,0,2G)$, which would need a current inside the beam screen."],"options":["$G(x,y,0)$","$G(-y,x,0)$","$G(y,x,0)$","$G(x,y,-z)$"],"id":"w10-p3-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: a small square with arrows across its edges for the normal components B1 and B2, illustrating net outflow. Right: the same square traversed counterclockwise with the tangential components labelled, illustrating circulation. No candidate field is drawn","caption":"The two tests to apply to each option: the divergence (net outflow per volume) and the curl (circulation per area). Inside the beam screen both must be zero.","file":"w10-p3-a"}},{"expected":["0","0","223*(y^2-x^2)/2"],"units":"T·m","kind":"vector","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Write the three component equations of $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf A=\\mathbf B$ with $A_1=0$, and integrate in $x$.","The second equation, $-\\partial_xA_3=B_2$, gives $A_3$ up to a function $g(y,z)$; the third, $\\partial_xA_2=B_3$, lets you take $A_2=0$. The first then fixes $\\partial_yg$."],"semantic":"curl","curlTarget":["223*y","223*x","0"],"firstComponentZero":true,"id":"w10-p3-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"A flowchart. The three component equations of curl A = B with A1 = 0 are in boxes: (1) d_y A3 - d_z A2 = B1, (2) -d_x A3 = B2, (3) d_x A2 = B3. Equations (2) and (3) are integrated in x, giving A3 and A2 up to functions g(y, z) and h(y, z), which are then substituted into equation (1)","caption":"Integrate equations (2) and (3) in $x$, keeping the leftover functions $g(y,z)$ and $h(y,z)$; equation (1) then says what $g_y-h_z$ must be.","file":"w10-p3-b"}},{"expected":"223*0.017^2/2","units":"Wb","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The flux in the $+y$ direction adds up $\\mathbf B\\cdot\\mathbf j=B_y$: a strip of width $dx$ at distance $x$ from the beam catches $B_y\\,dx$ webers per meter of coil.","On $y=0$, $B_y$ depends only on $x$; integrate over $0\\le x\\le0.017$ and $0\\le z\\le1$, or use the circulation of $\\mathbf A$ around the coil. Size check: positive, of order $10^{-2}$ Wb."],"id":"w10-p3-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the cross-section of the beam screen with the search coil along the positive x-axis from 0 to 17 mm and its normal n = j pointing up. Right: the coil seen face-on as a rectangle 0.017 m wide and 1 m long along the beam, with a thin strip of width dx at distance x shaded","caption":"The coil’s normal is $\\mathbf j$ (up), so only $B_y$ on the line $y=0$ matters. A strip of width $dx$ at distance $x$ from the beam catches $B_y(x,0)\\,dx$ webers per meter of coil.","file":"w10-p3-c"}}]},{"id":"w10-p4","questions":[{"expected":"x^2*z+y^2*z-2*z","units":"keV","kind":"potential","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Integrate one component at a time, keeping an unknown function of the remaining variables each time.","Integrate $2xz$ in $x$: $f=x^2z+g(y,z)$. Then $f_y=2yz$ forces $g=y^2z+h(z)$; match $f_z$ with $x^2+y^2-2$."],"id":"w10-p4-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: direction arrows of F = (2xz, 2yz, x^2 + y^2 - 2) in the plane y = 0, with the band |x| < square root of 2 shaded where F points down. Right: a three-step flowchart for successive integration: integrate F1 in x leaving g(y, z); match f_y to fix g up to h(z); match f_z to fix h up to a constant C","caption":"A potential $f$ increases in the direction of $\\mathbf F$. Build it one variable at a time, keeping an unknown function of the remaining variables at each step.","file":"w10-p4-a"}},{"expected":"(0.5^2*0-2*0)-(0.5^2*3-2*3)","units":"keV","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["For a gradient field, the work done is $f(\\text{end})-f(\\text{start})$, whatever the path.","The spiral starts at $\\mathbf c(0)=(0.5,0,3)$ and ends at $\\mathbf c(6\\pi)=(0.5,0,0)$. Size check: the published gain is several keV."],"id":"w10-p4-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: side view (x against z) of the spiral winding down from its start (0.5, 0, 3) to its end (0.5, 0, 0) at the ground, with a dashed straight path between the same two points. Right: top view of the spiral as the circle of radius 0.5 traversed three times, with start and end at the same point (0.5, 0)","caption":"The spiral starts at $\\mathbf c(0)$ and ends at $\\mathbf c(6\\pi)$, directly below it. For a gradient field, every path between these two points gives the same work.","file":"w10-p4-b"}},{"expected":"3","units":"","kind":"choice","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Two of these have nonzero curl, so they are out at once.","Zero curl is enough only if every loop in the domain can shrink to a point without leaving the domain."],"options":["The electrojet field $\\mathbf B$, on $\\mathbb R^3$ minus the current line","Earth’s rotational velocity $\\Omega(-y,x,0)$, on $\\mathbb R^3$","The charging pad’s induced field $\\tfrac b2(y,-x,0)$, on $\\mathbb R^3$","The point-source field $\\mathbf r/|\\mathbf r|^3$, on $\\mathbb R^3$ minus the origin"],"id":"w10-p4-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Two sketches under the question: can this loop shrink to a point without leaving the domain? Left: R^3 minus a straight line, with a loop around the line. Right: R^3 minus a point, with a loop around the point. No answer is marked","caption":"Zero curl gives a potential when every loop in the domain can shrink to a point without leaving it. Decide which domain each option lives on, and whether its loops can shrink.","file":"w10-p4-c"}}]},{"id":"w10-p5","questions":[{"expected":"47*10^12/(4*pi*6371000^2)*1000","units":"mW/m²","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["The outward flux through the whole sphere is $47$ TW $=4.7\\times10^{13}$ W.","Divide by the area $4\\pi R^2$ with $R=6.371\\times10^6$ m, then convert W/m$^2$ to mW/m$^2$. Size check: expect tens of mW/m$^2$, like the continental mean."],"id":"w10-p5-a","label":"a","hintFigure":{"alt":"A cross-section of Earth of radius R with outward arrows all around, a small patch dS on the surface with its outward normal n = r/R, and the notes: total outward flux 47 TW, area 4 pi R^2 with R = 6.371 times 10^6 m, average flux equals total over area, 1 W per square meter equals 1000 mW per square meter","caption":"The 47 TW is the total outward flux through the whole sphere; the average flux is that total divided by the area $4\\pi R^2$, with $R$ in meters.","file":"w10-p5-a"}},{"expected":"47*10^12/(4/3*pi*6371000^3)*10^9","units":"nW/m³","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["$\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf q=H$, so the Divergence Theorem gives $H$ times Earth’s volume $=47$ TW.","Earth’s volume is $\\tfrac43\\pi R^3$ with $R$ in meters; then convert W/m$^3$ to nW/m$^3$."],"id":"w10-p5-b","label":"b","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: a cross-section of the ball with arrows of q = (H/3)(x, y, z) pointing outward and growing longer with distance from the center. Right: a small cube dV with arrows leaving through its faces, labelled heat made H dV equals net outflow (div q) dV","caption":"Each small volume $dV$ makes heat $H\\,dV$, which leaves as the net outflow $(\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf q)\\,dV$. Adding up all the pieces gives the flux through the surface.","file":"w10-p5-b"}},{"expected":"73-25*(1-exp(-3.5))","units":"mW/m²","kind":"number","tolerance":1e-05,"hints":["Take a column of top area $A$. Only the top (outward flux $73A$) and the bottom (outward flux $-q_mA$) carry heat.","The volume integral is $A\\int_{-35}^0 2.5e^{z/10}\\,dz$; note that $2.5\\ \\mu$W/m$^3$ times $10$ km is $25$ mW/m$^2$."],"id":"w10-p5-c","label":"c","hintFigure":{"alt":"Left: the crustal column, 35 km deep with top area A, drawn as a box with outward normals: up on the top face, flux 73A; down on the bottom face, flux -q_m A; and horizontal on the sides, where q dot n = 0. Right: the heat production profile H(z) = 2.5 e^(z/10) microwatts per cubic meter from z = -35 km to 0, with the area under it shaded","caption":"Only the top and bottom faces carry heat, and the outward normal on the bottom points down. The shaded area under $H(z)$ is the heat made in the column per unit of top area.","file":"w10-p5-c"}}]}]}]};
